0.285 [Angelo Salese]

3DO FZ-1 R.E.A.L. Interactive Multiplayer (USA), Panasonic, 1993

Japanese multimedia console launched by Panasonic in 1993, representing the first commercial implementation of the 3DO platform. Designed for home entertainment, it featured a 12.5 MHz ARM60 processor, 2 MB of RAM, and a built-in CD-ROM drive. Its architecture was advanced for its time, boasting hardware acceleration for 2D/3D graphics, 16-bit stereo audio, and the ability to play full-motion video. Conceived as a multifunctional platform for games, interactive encyclopedias, educational films, and multimedia applications, it anticipated the modern concept of a console as a home entertainment hub.

