0.218 [rfka01]

3SIUD-1.1, <unknown>, 199?

It’s an AT-compatible PC motherboard based on the Intel 80386SX, manufactured by UMC (United Microelectronics Corporation). Designed for budget desktop systems in the late 1980s, it supports SIMM memory, ISA expansion slots, and typical features of the era. It was identified thanks to an uncommon BIOS string (“3SIUD-1.1”), likely customized by an OEM, and features a distinctive layout with SIMM slots positioned along the bottom edge of the board—a helpful trait for recognition. Ideal for retrocomputing and historical DOS system builds.

