The Intel 80386 (i386) processor-based motherboard with ALi M1419 chipset represents a type of computing hardware developed in the 1990s, at a time when IBM-compatible PCs were evolving toward greater integration and cost reduction. These motherboards were common for both desktop and embedded or industrial use. The ALi M1419 chipset, manufactured by ALi (Acer Laboratories Inc.), was one of the direct competitors to Intel and Chips and Technologies (C&T) chipsets, and was designed to support the basic functionality of an x86 system in an economical but efficient manner.

