$info=alphatpc16
$mame
0.219 [Carl, rfka01]

alphatronic PC-16, Triumph-Adler, 1985

The alphatronic PC16 is a somewhat crude mixture of a classic home computer and PC. With 128 KB RAM, a console housing and built-in Basic, it is similar to 8-bitters like the Schneider CPCs - but operating under MS-DOS via an external floppy disk drive also makes it suitable as an office computer. However, the shortage of working memory quickly becomes a problem. The external floppy can be plugged into a second drive via a socket on the underside of the device, which then handles the data traffic as well as its power supply via this connection. The controller is in the floppy case, as is the power supply for the computer. The latter is designed as an ordinary transformer, which gives the floppy a weight that you would normally only expect from a full-blown PC. On the right in the housing is a module that contains the video logic and the monitor connection. All in all, there is nothing reminiscent of a PC on the whole device: The connections do not correspond to any standard, you cannot install ISA plug-in cards, the keyboard has an idiosyncratic layout that some important keys (backspace, for example) are completely missing and even the MS-DOS has to be specially adapted his. Other boot disks refuse to work (source: https://homecomputermuseum.de/sammlung/detailansicht/comp/Computer/show/alphatronic-pc-16/).

