$info=alto2
$mame
0.154 [Juergen Buchmueller]

Alto-II, Xerox, 1977

The Xerox Alto computer, developed at Xerox PARC in 1973, was the first personal computer to use the desktop metaphor and to have a GUI (a graphical interface). The first project dates back to 1972 and is to be brought back to Butler Lampson, who produced a machine with 128 Kb of central memory (expandable to 512 Kb) and a removable 2,5 Mb removable hard disk. The dimensions were those of a small fridge. In addition to the Ethernet connection, the only HD output device was a vertically mounted CRT monochrome monitor (with cathode tube); the input devices were an alphanumeric keyboard, a three-button mouse, and optionally a kind of keyboard with only five keys, the "chord keyset" which, unlike the mouse, never had a great popularity. To tell the truth, the whole machine did not get a great deal of success, even though they produced a thousand units: because of the cheap price, the Alto computer did not manage to become a commercial product (source, traslated from Italian: http://www.storiaolivetti.it/template.asp?idOrd=0&idPercorso=608).

