$info=ampscarp
$mame
0.192 [Robbbert]

AMPS Car Phone, Motorola, 1998

While Motorola was developing a cellular phone, from 1968 to 1983 Bell Labs worked out a system called Advanced Mobile Phone System (AMPS), which became the first cellular network standard in the United States. The first system was successfully deployed in Chicago, Illinois, in 1979. Motorola and others designed and built the cellular phones for this and other cellular systems. AMPS is a first-generation cellular technology that uses separate frequencies, or “channels??, for each conversation. It therefore required considerable bandwidth for a large number of users. In general terms, AMPS was very similar to the older “0G?? Improved Mobile Telephone Service it replaced, but used considerably more computing power to select frequencies, hand off conversations to land lines, and handle billing and call setup (source: https://telephoneworld.org/cellular-phone-history/analog-cellular-amps-1g/).

