$info=anakonda
$mame
0.223 [Robbbert]

Anakonda, <unknown>, 1989

It’s an educational computer from the 1980s, developed in Yugoslavia for school and home use. Based on a microprocessor compatible with the Intel 8080 (or sometimes the Z80), it integrates a keyboard, TV video output, and cassette-based storage support. It boots directly into a ROM-resident BASIC interpreter—without a separate operating system—and includes simple applications and games for learning. Produced in small batches with local variations, it represents an example of grassroots computing in Eastern Europe during the Cold War.

