0.37b12 [Raphael Nabet, Robbbert]

All Purpose Electronic X-ray Computer (as described in 1957), Andrew Donald Booth, 1955

The description applies to the APEXC (for documents from 1957). The APEXC had 32 magnetic drum memory, but neither of RAM or ROM. For simplicity, both all commands, and operators were 32 bits long. The memory addresses had a length of 10 bits. Since it the usual instruction counter (PC) does not exist was the starting address of the next instruction in the commands included with. There were only 15 commands. To enter a keyboard with 32 keys or served the tape. The issue was also a printer or via paper tape (source, traslated from german: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/All_Purpose_Electronic_X-Ray_Computer).

