$info=arbv2
$mame
0.209 [anon]

ARB V2 Sargon 4.0, hack (Steve Braid), 2012

It is a self-made electronic board known as an "Auto Response Board" (ARB) in its second version, developed by experimenter Steve Braid. Based on a dedicated voice command recognition system, it uses Sargon 4.0 software to interpret simple words or phrases and trigger automatic responses, such as turning on lights or electrical devices. Designed as part of DIY and pioneering home automation, it combines digital electronics and rudimentary voice recognition, often with TTL components and microcontrollers of the time. It is an emblematic example of advanced pre-digital hardware hacking, made by enthusiasts in the 1980s to explore the potential of human-computer interaction in the home.

