$info=argo
$mame
0.142 [Robbbert]

Argo, <unknown>, 1986

It’s a Soviet educational system from the 1980s, designed to teach programming and automation in technical schools. Based on an Intel 8080 clone, it integrates a ROM-resident BASIC interpreter, a keyboard, TV video output, and cassette support. On startup, it loads the programming environment directly, without a separate operating system. Used in classroom settings for hands-on exercises in logic and control, it represents an example of computing applied to education during the Soviet era.

