$info=b16ex2
$mame
0.271 [Mike Stedman]

B16 EX-II, Hitachi, 1983?

The Hitachi B16 EX-II is a professional reel-to-reel videotape recorder developed by Hitachi in the 1980s for use in broadcasting and television production. It belongs to the 1” Type B format, an analog video standard widely used in Europe prior to the advent of digital technology. The B16 EX-II stands out for its image stability, recording quality, and mechanical robustness, which are characteristic of Hitachi products designed for television studios. Designed for continuous and reliable operation, it supports professional features such as frame-accurate editing, timecode, and synchronization with other video devices. It was commonly used for live broadcasts, program archiving, and video editing in broadcast environments. The EX-II model represents an upgrade over the original version, with improvements in electronics, signal handling, and operational interface. Despite being obsolete, the B16 EX-II is still valued by collectors and audiovisual archives for its build quality and fidelity in reproducing historical tapes.

