$info=backgamc
$mame
0.207 [hap, balrog, Lord Nightmare]

Backgammon Challenger, Fidelity Electronics, 1979

The Backgammon Challenger incorporated a Z80 A processor, 256 bytes of RAM and 4K of ROM with the program. It had a complete game board in the upper case. To the right of the dash was an LCD screen, two indicator diodes, and a 16-key keyboard. It was played with 30 chips (15 of each color), 2 game dice and a betting dice. The LCD screen had four digits by which instructions were received and the initial and final location of the chips was determined. In addition, in the middle of the screen (with two digits on each side) there were small diodes (DBL) that indicated the active player / computer (the diode representing the player was the one closest to the keyboard). The two indicator LEDs, which were located above the screen, lit up when the computer won (I WIN) or lost (I LOSE). The keyboard, located below the screen, had 10 number keys (0 to 9) and 6 game keys: "EN", "CL", "GM", RE "," PB "and" PV " (source, traslated from Spanish: https://retroordenadoresorty.blogspot.com/2019/05/fidelity-backgammon-challenger-bkc-1978.html?m=1).

