0.271 [David Haywood, TeamEurope, Peter Wilhelmsen]

Bistro Kids: Boku mo Watashi mo Kok-san! (Japan), Sega Toys / SSD Company LTD, 2001

Plug-and-play device designed for TVs, tailored for children and inspired by the world of cooking and dining—transforming players into budding chefs through a physical controller shaped like a kitchen utensil (such as a spatula or ladle). This educational and engaging gameplay revolves around mini-games focused on preparing, cooking, and serving dishes, all synchronized with vibrant animations, original voiceovers, and immersive sound effects. The experience includes progressively challenging levels, along with simple scoring systems and goals to encourage coordination, logic, and role-playing skills. Featuring a sturdy, ergonomically designed build—typical of Japan’s “taikan” systems—it delivers an active, fully immersive play experience—no additional console required, making it perfect for family fun and discovering the joy of culinary creativity.

