BK 0011, Elektronika, 1989

It is a Soviet computer released in 1989, based on the KR580VM80A processor (a clone of the Intel 8080). Designed for educational and home use, it features 32 KB of RAM, composite video output for a TV, an integrated keyboard, and support for audio cassettes. It boots directly into a BASIC interpreter stored in ROM and has a compact design with a built-in cassette recorder. Widely distributed in Soviet schools and households as a local alternative to Western systems, it represents one of the final models in the BK series.

