0.144 [DATAsoft]

BK-8T, <unknown>, 1987

Soviet microcomputer from the 1980s, designed for educational use, office applications, and programming, based on an 8-bit architecture with a Z80 processor or its compatible counterpart. Equipped with a built-in keyboard, a monochromatic alphanumeric display capable of 24 lines × 80 columns, support for floppy disks, and serial/parallel connectivity options. It supports programming languages such as BASIC and Pascal, and can run applications for word processing, calculations, data management, and telematic communications. Featuring a command-line interface in Russian, manual configuration of system parameters, and the ability to function as a telecommunications terminal. Widely used in schools, technical institutes, and government environments, it stands as a significant example of autonomous computing in the Soviet Union during the pre-PC era of standardized technology.

