$info=blitzs
$mame
0.128 [Miodrag Milanović]

Blic, <unknown>, 1990

It’s an unofficial clone of the ZX Spectrum 48K, produced in Yugoslavia in the early 1990s. Built using locally sourced or Eastern Bloc components, it replicates the original architecture with a Zilog Z80 microprocessor (or equivalent), 48 KB of RAM, and near-complete software compatibility with the British Spectrum. It features a compact plastic case—often in bright colors—a membrane keyboard, and an RF video output for connecting to household televisions. Some versions include minor hardware modifications, such as localized keyboard layouts or joystick support. Designed for home and educational use in a context of limited access to Western hardware, it exemplifies the grassroots “garage” cloning trend common in Eastern Europe during the post-communist transition.

