$info=bridgec
$mame
0.221 [hap, Sean Riddle]

Bridge Challenger, Fidelity Electronics, 1979

Computer with the card game "bridge" manufactured by Fidelity Electronics (USA). It was part of the Challenger series with which de la Fidelity obtained its first victory (of four in a row) in the 1980 world chess championship. The company became a world leader in the manufacture of chess computers although it also manufactured others with different board games, including card games "bridge". The first Bridge Challenger (BRC) (this computer) was put on sale in 1978 (it was on sale for a short time), being replaced the following year by the Advanced Bridge Challenger (UBC). In the company's 1979 brochure, the Fidelity Challenger series had six computers: three chess computers (the Chess Challenger 7, the Chess Challenger 10, and the Voice Chess Challenger) and three others with board games (the Bridge Challenger, Checker Challenger, and Backgammon Challenger). Later (1980-1983), the Bridge Challenger had three more versions, the Voice Bridge Challenger (VBRC), the Bridge Challenger II (BV2) and the Bridge Challenger III (BV3) (source, traslated from Spanish: https://retroordenadoresorty.blogspot.com/2020/12/fidelity-bridge-challenger-bcr-1987.html?m=1).

