$info=byte
$mame
0.128 [Miodrag Milanović]

PEVM Byte, BEMZ, 1990

Compact and modular microcomputer system, designed in the 1980s in Eastern Europe for educational and professional use, based on an 8-bit architecture (often compatible with CP/M or similar systems). It includes a built-in keyboard, expansion slots, serial/parallel ports, and the ability to connect to an external monitor or TV. Intended for programming, data management, and light industrial automation, it stands out for its robustness, compatibility with peripherals of the era, and functional design. Suitable for technical schools, laboratories, and hobbyist users, it represents an example of a "cloned" computer from the pre-PC era, developed to foster local technological self-sufficiency.

