$info=cbm620
$mame
0.36 [Curt Coder]

CBM 620, Commodore Business Machines, 1983

The Commodore CBM 610 is part of the series 600/700, the new powerful line of professional equipment developed and produced by Commodore starting in 1982, also known as CBM-II series. Computers in this series were equipped with MOS 6509 CPU compatible with the 6502 to 2MHz, 80-column display modes, Basic 4.0, SID sound chip (the same as the C64), a serial RS-232 interface and the usual IEEE-488 floppy disk drives and printers. The models 710 and CBM CBM 720 were characterized by a case with integrated monitor and separate keyboard, very similar to that of the Commodore 8032SK while the models 610 and 620 were compact and free of the monitor (source italian traslated: http://myretrocomputing.altervista.org/commodorecbm610/index.php).

