$info=cbm8296dgv_de
$mame
0.157 [Mike Naberezny]

CBM 8296-D GV? (Germany), Commodore Business Machines, 1984

The CBM-II series was the line of professional computers developed by Commodore as an evolution of the previous PET/CBM series. This line also included the?Commodore 8296?and the?Commodore 8296-D?which are considered the descendants of the "old"?CBM8032?due to the many similarities they had with this machine. The first difference that catches the eye compared to the previous series is the beauty of the new cases, where before there were only right angles, sharp edges and a total lack of ergonomics, now there are curves, harmonious shapes and ease of use.  Surely these are some of the most beautiful computers ever made in terms of aesthetics. The CBM 8296-D represented the evolutionary peak and at the same time the stopping point of the PET/CBM II series.  It was in fact one of the most powerful 8bit computers available at the time: the already remarkable characteristics of the CBM 8296 were added 2 5.25-inch drives mounted in the upper part of the chassis capable of archiving up to 1Mb of data on normal disks DS/DD, a true record. The only drawback was the fact that since the file system was not hierarchical it was not possible to organize the file in directories and therefore you had very large disks but with endless lists of files (source, translated from Italian: http://myretrocomputing.altervista.org/commodorecbm8296/index.php).

