$info=cd32
$mame
0.132 [incog, Fabio Priuli]

Amiga CD32 (PAL), Commodore, 1993

The Amiga CD32, styled 'CD32' (code-named 'Spellbound'), was the first 32-bit CD-ROM based video game console released in western Europe, Australia, Canada and Brazil. It was first announced at the Science Museum in London, United Kingdom on 16 July 1993, and was released in September of the same year. The CD32 is based on Commodore's Advanced Graphics Architecture chipset, and is of similar specification to the Amiga 1200 computer. Using 3rd-party devices, it is possible to upgrade the CD32 with keyboard, floppy drive, hard drive and mouse, turning it into an Amiga 1200 personal computer. A hardware MPEG decompression module for playing Video CD was also available. The CD32 managed to secure over 38% of the CD-ROM market in the UK (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amiga_CD32).

