$info=channelf
$mame
0.37b6 [Frank Palazzolo, Juergen Buchmueller]

Channel F, Fairchild, 1976

The Fairchild Channel F is a game console released by Fairchild Semiconductor in August 1976 at the retail price of $169.95. It has the distinction of being the first programmable ROM cartridge-based video game console, and the first console to use a microprocessor. It was launched as the Video Entertainment System, or VES, but when Atari released their VCS the next year, Fairchild renamed its machine. By 1977, the Fairchild Channel F had sold 250,000 units and was second-place behind the VCS (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairchild_Channel_F).

