$info=conc
$mame
0.219 [hap, Berger]

Conchess (standard, set 1), Consumenta Computer / Waltham Electronics, 1982

Conchess was manufactured by Consumenta, a German company based in Munich. The brand name "Conchess" is made up of Consumenta and Chess. The devices were manufactured by Wallharn Electronics, a factory for hi-fi equipment in Ireland. Consumenta was not unknown on this market, because up until the early 1980s this company had taken over the distribution of chess computers from Fidelity Electronics. Consumenta owners Peter-Ingolf Gericke and Klaus Heyne became aware of Conchess after seeing the success of a new experimental chess program called Princhess 2.9 from Sweden, programmed by Ulf Rathsman. This program had just finished second at the 1981 World Microcomputer Chess Championships in Travem?nde, Germany. Ulf Rathsman approved the use of Princhess and the Conchess chess computer brand was launched in 1982. After the bankruptcy of the conchess manufacturer Waltham Electronics in 1983, the Swedish company System Integration (Stockholm) took over the interests of program author Ulf Rathsman and employed him permanently. As a work colleague he was given the young Lars Hjorth. Conchess only lasted until 1990, but during that short period they built beautiful modular chess boards and chess modules (source, traslated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php/Conchess).

DRIVER:
chess/conchess.cpp

