$info=concept
$mame
0.70 [Raphael Nabet]

Concept, Corvus Systems, 1982

The Corvus supported making documents with intermixed pictures and text: which was very well presented for a 1982 PC. It also had other applications accessed by function keys. Business and pleasure software, mostly practical software applications. Competing computers had only simple text and no desktop arrangement. The Corvus supported a runtime Pascal interpreter. You could interrupt your programs, edit them, and keep running. Pascal was advanced / not just BASIC. The manuals had all the source code printed just in case you wanted to read up on your application to change it. Not as innovative as mainframe software / but very innovative for personal use technology at the time, usable by a computer newbie or a programmer. (Pascal was not comparable to Lisp / but Pascal was easily understood and highly functional). The small PC market disappeared as technology was rapidly released: things were no longer small (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corvus_Systems#The_Concept).

DRIVER:
concept/concept.cpp

