$info=digilog320
$mame
0.240 [Bitsavers]

320, Digilog, 1988

It’s a serial protocol analyzer from the 1980s, based on a Z80 CPU and designed to monitor RS-232 communications. Unlike more advanced models, it features a single serial channel and limited resources (16 KB of RAM and 8 KB of ROM), sufficient to capture and display data streams in real time in hexadecimal or ASCII format. Lacking an integrated screen, it sends its output to a remote terminal connected via a serial port. Used for basic debugging, serial link verification, and embedded development, it represents an entry-level version of the analysis tools commonly found in technical labs during the pre-USB era.

