$info=dms86
$mame
0.137 [Robbbert]

DMS-86, Digital Microsystems, 1982

An 8-bit portable device based on the Z80 architecture, designed for learning and developing computer skills. It features an integrated keyboard, an LCD display, and supports programming in languages such as BASIC and assembly. Created for educational use, it allows users to write, run, and store programs—often via audio cassettes or external memory—delivering an experience similar to early 1980s home computers.

