$info=dsb46
$mame
0.150 [Al Kossow, Curt Coder]

DSB-4/6, Davidge, 198?

The Davidge DSB-4/6 is an AT-compatible PC motherboard produced in the United States in the latter half of the 1980s. Based on the Intel 80286 processor, it was designed for professional desktop systems and supports up to 16 MB of RAM—a substantial capacity for its time—featuring 30-pin SIMM slots, 8- and 16-bit ISA expansion slots, and integrated floppy and hard disk controllers. Characterized by a modular and reliable layout, it includes jumpers for advanced memory and clock configuration and is commonly paired with either Phoenix or Award BIOS, depending on the version. Although not an official IBM clone, it offers full compatibility with MS-DOS and contemporary business applications. Its robustness and scalability made it well-suited for corporate workstations and high-end embedded systems prior to the widespread adoption of the 386.

