$info=dtkerso
$mame
0.222 [rfka01]

PC-XT-Clones with DTK/ERSO-BIOS, DTK Group, 198?

It is a series of PC XT clones manufactured in Taiwan during the 1980s by DTK Computer in collaboration with ERSO (Electronics Research and Service Organization), a Taiwanese government institute. Based on the original IBM PC XT architecture, these systems use an Intel 8088 processor, eight 8-bit ISA slots, and up to 640 KB of RAM. The BIOS, jointly developed by DTK and ERSO, is identifiable by the "DTK/ERSO" string and provides basic functionality for floppy booting, memory management, and hard disk support (in advanced versions). These machines were among the first mass-produced PC clones in Asia, designed to lower costs and promote computing adoption in emerging markets. While fully compatible with MS-DOS and contemporary software, they occasionally feature minor hardware deviations from the IBM standard—particularly in floppy disk controllers and memory mapping.

