$info=duet16
$mame
0.195 [rfka01, Carl]

Duet-16, Panafacom (Panasonic/Fujitsu), 1983

It’s a desktop computer released in 1983, developed jointly by Panasonic and Fujitsu under the shared brand **Panafacom**. Based on the 16-bit Intel 8086 processor, it was one of the earliest Japanese systems compatible with the PC architecture—but not with the IBM PC standard. It features up to 768 KB of RAM, integrated floppy disk drives, and a proprietary operating system (**Panafacom OS** or **Human68k** in some configurations), along with support for CP/M-86. Designed for professional and engineering use, it offered advanced performance for its time, with a modular design and serial/parallel interfaces for peripherals. Although it never achieved global commercial success, it remains a notable example of Japanese innovation in the pre-widespread PC-compatible era.

