$info=dvbslan
$mame
0.218 [rfka01]

Book-Size LAN station, Datavan, 199?

It’s a compact “book-size” computer (similar to a laptop but without an integrated screen), marketed in the 1990s as a lightweight network station or an early thin client. Designed for business environments, it features a motherboard with a 386SX or 486SLC CPU, limited memory, serial/parallel ports, and—distinctively—an integrated Ethernet network card (often based on chipsets like the NE2000), which was rare on consumer systems of the era. It lacks a hard drive; booting typically occurs from a floppy disk or, in advanced setups, over the network (as a diskless workstation). Intended for terminal services, resource sharing, or access to centralized servers, it represents an early example of network-oriented computing in professional settings.

