$info=dvk_ksm
$mame
0.156 [Sergey Svishchev]

DVK KSM, USSR, 1986

It is a Soviet scientific and industrial computer introduced in 1986, part of the DVK family (*Dialogovaya Vychislitelnaya Mashina* – “Interactive Computing Machine”). Based on a locally produced PDP-11 clone (the KM1801VM1 CPU, compatible with the LSI-11 series), it features a 16-bit architecture, up to 4 MB of RAM (expandable via external modules), and runs either RTOS-11 or the native DVK operating system. It supports peripherals such as floppy and hard disk drives and video terminals. Designed for use in laboratories, research institutes, and industrial automation, it was widely deployed in academic and government settings across the Soviet Union. The KSM variant (*Kontrol’no-Schetnaya Mashina* – “Control and Computing Machine”) included extensions for real-time data acquisition and process control, making it suitable for monitoring and managing technological systems.

