$info=ec1841
$mame
0.137 [Miodrag Milanović]

EC-1841, <unknown>, 1987

It is an IBM PC/XT-compatible computer produced in the Soviet Union (and later in Russia) starting in 1987. Based on a locally manufactured clone of the Intel 8088 processor (the KR1810VM88), it replicates the original IBM PC hardware architecture, including an 8-bit ISA bus, floppy and hard disk controllers, and up to 640 KB of RAM. It is designed to run MS-DOS—often localized variants such as DOS-86 or RDOS—and PC XT-compatible software. Widely deployed in government, educational, and industrial settings, it represents one of the Soviet Union’s major large-scale efforts to clone a Western computer system during the Cold War, aimed at reducing reliance on imported technology.

