EC-1842, <unknown>, 1989

It is an IBM PC/AT-compatible computer produced in the Soviet Union (and later in Russia) starting in 1989. Based on a locally manufactured clone of the Intel 80286 processor (the KR1842VM286), it replicates the original PC AT architecture, featuring a 16-bit ISA bus, support for extended memory (up to several megabytes), integrated floppy and hard disk controllers, and a configurable clock speed. It is designed to run MS-DOS—often localized variants such as RDOS or DOS-86—and PC AT-compatible business applications. Widely used in government agencies, technical institutes, and research centers, it represents a significant step beyond XT clones, aimed at narrowing the technological gap with the West during the final phase of the Cold War.

