$info=ec1845
$mame
0.137 [Miodrag Milanović]

EC-1845, <unknown>, 1989

It is an IBM PS/2 Model 30-compatible computer produced in the Soviet Union (and later in Russia) starting in 1989. Based on a locally manufactured clone of the Intel 80286 processor (the KR1842VM286), it features a hybrid architecture that blends PC AT elements with select PS/2 characteristics—such as an AT-bus (not Micro Channel) and an integrated video controller compatible with VGA or EGA. It supports several megabytes of RAM, 3.5" floppy drives, hard disks, and a DIN keyboard connector. Designed to run MS-DOS and advanced graphical software of the era, it was deployed in technical, academic, and government environments as an evolution of earlier XT/AT clones. It represents a Soviet effort to align with IBM’s newer standards while maintaining compatibility with existing hardware.

