$info=ec1847
$mame
0.152 [crazyc]

EC-1847, <unknown>, 1990

It is an IBM PS/2-compatible computer produced in the Soviet Union (and later in Russia) starting in 1990. Based on a locally manufactured clone of the Intel 80386DX processor (the KR1847VM386), it represents one of the first Soviet 32-bit clones. It features an advanced architecture for its time, including a 16-bit ISA bus (not Micro Channel, despite the PS/2 reference), support for extended memory up to several megabytes, an integrated VGA controller, 3.5" floppy drives, and IDE or MFM hard disks. Designed to run MS-DOS, DR-DOS, and—in some configurations—early versions of OS/2 or localized UNIX-like systems, it was primarily used in research centers, military institutes, and large state-owned enterprises. The system embodies the peak of Soviet efforts to clone Western hardware before the USSR’s collapse, with a clear goal of closing the technological gap in scientific and industrial fields.

