EC-1849, <unknown>, 1993

It is a desktop computer developed in Russia in the early 1990s and marketed starting in 1993. Based on a locally produced clone of the Intel 80486DX processor (the KR1849VM486), it represents one of the last and most advanced systems in the EC (Elektronnye Cifrovye) series, inspired by IBM standards. It features an extended PC/AT architecture with a 16-bit ISA bus, support for up to 64 MB of RAM, an integrated VGA controller, serial and parallel ports, and an IDE interface for hard drives. Designed to run MS-DOS, Windows 3.1, and—in some configurations—local operating systems such as DOS-86 or Russian Linux variants (e.g., ALT Linux later on), it was used by government agencies, universities, and enterprises transitioning toward modern computing.

