UC4915 A AIO, Elitegroup, 199?

It’s a Socket 7-compatible PC motherboard produced in the 1990s. Based on a VIA chipset, it supports Intel Pentium MMX and AMD K6/K6-2 processors. It features “All-in-One” (AIO) integration, with IDE, audio, video (often based on S3 or Trident chipsets), and—on some versions—network controllers built directly onto the board, reducing the need for expansion cards. It provides 30-pin SIMM and/or 168-pin DIMM memory slots (depending on the revision), PCI and ISA expansion slots, and AGP bus support in more advanced configurations. Designed for budget and multimedia desktop systems of the post-Pentium era, it exemplifies the industry’s shift toward integrated platforms typical of the late 1990s.

