$info=ec65
$mame
0.133 [Miodrag Milanović, Robbbert]

EC-65, Elektor, 1985

It is a homebrew computer featured in the Dutch magazine Elektor in 1985, designed for electronics enthusiasts and hobbyists. Based on the MOS 6502 microprocessor, it employs a modular architecture with interchangeable boards, allowing users to assemble it themselves using schematics and kits provided by the magazine. It includes support for a keyboard, composite video output (often via an RF modulator), a cassette tape interface for program loading, and up to 64 KB of RAM. The system boots either a ROM-resident monitor or a custom BASIC interpreter and is intended for learning programming, digital electronics, and developing small embedded projects. It stands as a quintessential example of the 1980s European DIY (Do-It-Yourself) movement.

