It is an enhanced version of the 1985 homebrew project, released in 1986 and aimed at electronics and programming enthusiasts. Still based on the MOS 6502 microprocessor, it features expandable RAM up to 64 KB and includes a ROM with an advanced BASIC interpreter, a system monitor, and development utilities. Its modular architecture supports the addition of peripherals such as floppy disk drives, serial interfaces, or graphics cards, while initial program loading typically occurs via cassette tape. Designed for education and experimentation, it represents a practical and accessible evolution of the European DIY movement of the era.

