$info=ecsum386
$mame
0.210 [AJR]

UM386 (Rev 1.1), Elitegroup Computer Systems, 1992

It is an IBM PC–compatible motherboard based on the Intel 80386DX processor, targeted at entry-level to mid-range desktop systems. It supports a 16-bit ISA bus, up to 16 MB of RAM (via 30-pin SIMM modules), and features integrated controllers for floppy disk drives, IDE hard disks, and serial/parallel ports. It lacks VESA Local Bus slots and onboard L2 cache, reflecting the cost-conscious design typical of Taiwanese manufacturers of the era. Intended for upgrading older platforms or for use by independent system builders, it offered a reliable and affordable solution for running operating systems such as MS-DOS, Windows 3.1, or early Linux distributions on 32-bit hardware.

