$info=ed486vl3h
$mame
0.218 [rfka01]

486VL3H, Edom, 199?

It is an IBM PC–compatible motherboard based on an Intel 80486 processor (likely a 486SX or 486DX2), designed for budget desktop systems in the early-to-mid 1990s. It uses a VESA Local Bus (VL-Bus) chipset, offering improved graphics and I/O performance compared to ISA-only architectures. The board features 16-bit ISA slots, VESA Local Bus slots for fast video cards or controllers, support for 32–64 MB of RAM via 30-pin SIMM modules, and integrated controllers for floppy drives, IDE hard disks, and serial/parallel ports. Targeted at system integrators and small computer workshops, it represents a transitional solution between the 386 era and the arrival of the Pentium, suitable for running MS-DOS, Windows 3.1x, or early versions of Windows 95—albeit with hardware limitations.

