$info=elecbowl
$mame
0.157 [hap, Kevin Horton]

Electronic Bowling (Marx), Marx, 1978

It is one of the earliest dedicated handheld electronic games, released in 1978 and inspired by bowling. Housed in a compact plastic case, it uses a simple LED matrix or a seven-segment display to simulate the lane and ball movement. The player controls the angle and power of the throw using buttons, aiming to knock down all the pins within a limited number of throws. Battery-powered, it delivers a self-contained and immediate gaming experience typical of the first generation of electronic entertainment devices aimed at the home market. It stands as a quintessential example of the wave of single-purpose electronic games that preceded the era of LCD screens and programmable consoles.

