$info=elt386sx
$mame
0.215 [rfka01]

ELT-386SX-160BE, Elitegroup, 199?

It is an IBM PC–compatible motherboard based on the Intel 80386SX processor, designed for budget desktop systems in the early 1990s. Operating at typical clock speeds of 16–20 MHz, it supports up to 16 MB of RAM via 30-pin SIMM modules and integrates standard controllers for floppy drives, IDE hard disks, and serial/parallel ports. It relies exclusively on 16-bit ISA expansion slots, with no support for local buses such as VESA or PCI, reflecting a cost-conscious, entry-level design. Intended for running MS-DOS, Windows 3.1, and early Linux distributions on affordable 32-bit hardware, it represents a typical Asian-made clone of the era, widely used by independent system builders and small businesses.

