$info=epc
$mame
0.190 [Joakim Larsson Edström, Joel Tegner]

Ericsson PC, Ericsson Information System, 1985

The computer had a brown phosphor screen with yellow text that was really easy to read and comfortable for the eye. It was, of course, equipped with an Intel 8088 processor of 4.77 MHz in a conventional PC mode. The computer was quite successful and was followed by an AT clone (286th) and a portable variant. The desktop computer I have in the museum is the PC model which has an expanded numeric keypad with numerical part as an addition. Normally it was delivered with a standard PC keyboard. There were versions with two 5? floppys without hard disk, but the most common one had 10M hard drive and one floppy (actually a PC-XT clone). The RAM was 256k but could be expanded to 640k with a special plug-in card. The computer was later equipped with color screen according to IBM's CGA standard. Ericsson did a great job in the US with its color PC. Competition in the United States was probably not what it had meant. It was not easy to come in with an entirely unknown brand and compete with well-known and established IBM. One might regard the US venture as a catastrophic error assessment. You probably did not really understand the difficulty of signing in a new market with an unknown brand. The Americans are quite conservative and trust their domestic brands (source, translated by the Swedes: http://www.pc-museum.com/ericsson/index.htm).

DRIVER:
ericsson/eispc.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
ibm5150.xml

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon and machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection).

