0.274 [TeamEurope, David Haywood]

Minna no Tetris (Japan), Epoch, 2007

According to the brain-related research of Professor Osamu Shinohara of Tokyo University, playing Tetris stimulates the frontal lobe of the brain and may prevent dementia. NCS is unsure if that little bit of information is a ringing endorsement of Tetris since observing the mating ritual of the humpback whale is probably just as stimulating, in terms of the frontal lobe. In any case, Tetris is a ubiquitous game and we've played hundreds of variants, clones, public domain iterations, and sequels through the years. Our frontal lobes have been stimulated like the dickens. Just like Jello however, there's always room for Tetris and NCS imports Minna no Tetris which is a self-contained TV game which features 11 versions of the game from the classic one-player version to a competitive 2-player rendition to a version that features big blocks which is perfect for the kids (source: https://www.ebay.com/itm/192535566425).

