0.190 [Joakim Larsson Edström, Joel Tegner, FakeShemp]

Ericsson Portable PC, Ericsson Information System, 1985

The Ericsson Portable PC had a brungul plasma screen with 80x25 characters and was a full PC clone except for the graphics that were a bit special. Built-in was an 80-character thermal printer. Built-in modem was also available as an option. In addition, there was a strange "bottom plate" to the computer containing network cards and some other kolijox. The computer was sometimes called the advent calendar because there were so many different gaps on the one that could be opened. On the computer was an Intel 8088 processor running 4.77 MHz on common PC manages. 256-512 kB RAM, 1x360 kB 5? "FDD, no hard drive. The graphics were 640 x 400 CGA. Power supply power for 220 volts was built-in. However, the ability to drive a battery was missing! Maybe unusual for a laptop (source, translated by the Swedes: http://www.pc-museum.com/ericsson/index.htm).

