Eurit 30, Ascom, 1996

It’s a professional digital telephone released in 1996, designed for use with ISDN-based business phone systems (PABX). Featuring an alphanumeric display, an extended keypad with programmable function keys, multi-line support, and caller ID (CLIP), it offers advanced features such as call forwarding, three-way conferencing, an internal directory, and integration with voicemail systems. Tailored for office and corporate environments, it stands out for its reliability, audio quality, and seamless integration with the digital telecommunications infrastructure of the time.

