It’s a professional workstation released in 1993, based on the 64-bit MIPS R4400 processor and designed for CAD, 3D modeling, and rendering applications. Part of NEC’s Engineering WorkStation (EWS) series, it runs the EWS4800-UX operating system, a proprietary variant of UNIX System V. It features a high-performance RISC architecture, integrated advanced graphics (often with a dedicated 2D/3D accelerator), support for large amounts of RAM, and SCSI storage. Targeted at engineers, designers, and technical studios, it represents a high-end solution from the pre-Intel workstation era, focused on stability, computational power, and compatibility with both Japanese and international engineering software.

