$info=expchess
$mame
0.261 [hap, Sean Riddle]

Explorer Chess, SciSys / LogiSoft, 1983

It’s a dedicated chess computer released in 1983, designed for both beginner and advanced players. Based on an 8-bit microprocessor (typically a 6502 or Z80), it offers multiple difficulty levels, game modes (play, analysis, puzzles), and a physical chessboard with pressure sensors and magnetic pieces. An LED display shows moves, timing, and system messages. Intended for home use and training, it represents a typical example of early commercial chess computers from the pre-32-bit era.

