0.154 [Robbbert, Anna Wu]

FC-100, Goldstar, 1982

It’s a home computer released in Korea in 1982, based on the Zilog Z80 microprocessor and designed for home and educational use. Compatible with the CP/M operating system, it features 64 KB of RAM, video output for television (RF or composite), a cassette interface for program loading, and expansion ports. The system boots into a ROM-resident BASIC interpreter and was primarily used in schools and by Korean hobbyists as a local alternative to Western models of the era.

