$info=feasx
$mame
0.267 [Berger]

Elite A/S Challenger (experimental), Fidelity Computer Products, 1985

The programs of Chess Challenger as Prestige II, Chess Challenger as Elite-S, Elite Private and Chess Challenger as Elegance are closely related. There was initially a pre-Budapest version for the Challenger as Elite-S, then a Budapest version and finally a revised Budapest version for the Elite and Prestige II, which ran at a clock frequency of 3.2 to 3.6 MHz in the former and 4.0 MHz in the latter. The program was revised for Glasgow and is now being sold in a revised version. In the Elegance it runs at 3.6 MHz, in the Elite-S mostly at 4.0 MHz and in the Elite Private at least (?) 5.0 MHz. There also seem to be slightly different program variants in circulation. The practical effects of all these subtle differences are usually somewhat overestimated, the programs only differ in details, although further development has not only brought improvements. The speed increases only result in a factor of five to ten. Half-train increased calculation depth and so only fractions of that are to be estimated here. If you really want to, you can have your "older" Elite-S upgraded to the latest standard at Fidelity in Mülheim for around DM 250.00. I have no personal experience of the possibilities offered by the so-called "resonator" - whatever that means - and the conversion by a third party, and I do not intend to look into it in more detail, as the achievable benefit is quite modest... (source, translated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php?title=Fidelity_Elite_A/S_Challenger).

DRIVER:
fidelity/elite.cpp

