$mame
0.142 [Barry Rodewald]

FM-Towns II HR, Fujitsu, 1992

It is a Japanese multimedia computer released in 1992, developed for the home market and based on the x86 architecture. Designed for gaming, entertainment, and multimedia applications, it features a 33 MHz Intel 80486 processor, native CD-ROM support, high-quality stereo audio (16-bit PCM), and advanced graphics with resolutions up to 768×512 pixels and thousands of colors. It includes joystick ports, RGB and RF video outputs, and a proprietary DOS-based operating system optimized for fast booting from optical discs. It was particularly praised for its FM Towns games and interactive encyclopedias, representing one of the first consumer systems with integrated CD-ROM capabilities.

