Freedom 200, Liberty Electronics, 1983

It is an intelligent terminal released in 1983, based on an 8-bit architecture (typically Z80 or 8085) and designed for professional and industrial environments. Equipped with a text display, integrated keyboard, and RS-232 serial interface, it runs firmware stored in ROM to communicate with central systems such as mainframes or minicomputers. It supports standard protocols of the era (including VT100/VT52) and offers features like local editing, buffering, and data transmission. Widely used in businesses, banks, and computing centers, this terminal combines reliability, compatibility, and ease of operation within a rugged design tailored for remote access to legacy infrastructure.

