$info=future32a
$mame
0.281 [Olivier Galibert, Plamen Mihaylov]

Future 32α, Sord, 1989

It’s a Japanese computer released in 1989, based on the 32-bit Intel 80386DX processor and featuring an advanced architecture for its time. Designed for professional and scientific use, it supports up to 16 MB of RAM, includes standard ISA expansion slots alongside a proprietary S-Bus, features an integrated VGA video controller, and offers support for both SCSI and IDE hard drives. It runs operating systems such as MS-DOS, OS/2, and UNIX System V, and is intended for software development, automation, and technical applications. Its modular design and high performance place it among the high-end workstations produced in Japan in the late 1980s.

