$info=fxmcr165
$mame
0.172 [hap, Sean Riddle]

FX-Micom R-165 (rev. A), Gakken, 1981

It is a Japanese home computer released in 1981, powered by the RCA CDP1802 microprocessor (also known as the CMOS 1802). Designed for educational and hobbyist use, it features 2 KB of RAM, a built-in seven-segment display for output, a compact alphanumeric keyboard, and support for loading programs via audio cassette. It boots into a resident monitor stored in ROM, enabling direct input and execution of machine-language code or a simple BASIC interpreter. Marketed by a well-known Japanese publishing company, it was available either as a kit or preassembled version, complete with instructional manuals aimed at teaching programming and electronics.

