FX-Micom R-165 (older), Gakken, 1981

Japanese educational microcomputer from the 1980s, designed to introduce students and enthusiasts to programming and electronics. Built around an 8-bit architecture with a Z80 processor or similar, it features an alphanumeric keyboard, a seven-segment display or LED output, and expansion ports for additional modules or TV connectivity. Programmable in BASIC or machine language, it allows users to perform simple calculations, play educational games, and build small electronic projects. Created for pedagogical purposes, this device encourages hands-on learning of computer science and how computers work—typical of the Gakken approach: accessible, modular, and focused on "learning by doing".

