$info=galghdmi
$mame
0.286 [TeamEurope]

Galaga HDMI TV Plug & Play, <unknown>, 2021

An unofficial, budget-friendly TV gaming device (not manufactured by Bandai Namco) marketed by Asian OEM companies between the 2010s and 2020s under unauthorized licenses or as modified clones of the original Galaga game (Namco, 1981). It features a compact design shaped like a controller or mini-console, complete with a built-in HDMI cable for direct connection to a television ("Plug & Play"). The device includes a simplified version of the arcade game with 2D graphics inspired by the original—featuring a spaceship, enemy formations, and double-shuttle power-ups—but with significant technical compromises: limited resolution, low-quality sampled audio, and the absence of authentic arcade features. Some versions even add non-original variations (extra levels, modified enemies) to circumvent copyright restrictions. Power is supplied via a USB port or an included adapter. Targeted at discount markets and low-cost online sales, this product exemplifies the typical Chinese industry of unauthorized arcade clones—lacking official licensing from Namco or Bandai Namco. For an authentic gaming experience, we recommend purchasing official versions such as Galaga on Namco Museum compilations or authorized devices like the Namco Arcade Stick.

