$info=galileo
$mame
0.221 [hap, Berger]

Kasparov Galileo (v1.4), Saitek / Heuristic Software, 1988

This chess computer is made of high-grade wood and it has a full tournament size playing surface. The board itself is 20 1/2 x 20 1/2 inches. Each square contains a sensor which can detect the presence of a piece on it, which means that you can play your moves exactly like you do in a normal game of chess. It has 32 levels of skill, including special levels for tournament chess, speed games, analysis, problem solving, etc. You can take back any number of moves, replay the entire game or watch the computer analyze a position while it is computing a move. If you need help at any time during a game the Galileo is always prepared to suggest a move for you. If you prefer to not use the computer and instead play a normal game against a real opponent, just close the front door as shown in the picture above and you have a beautiful regular chess board. The computer runs on four C batteries (new ones included) and performs just as it did twenty years ago. One of the white rooks (castles) has some small tooth marks (puppy) in it as I've tried to show in a picture above, but doesn't in the least inhibit its function. You won't be ashamed to show off this beautiful set in your home (source: https://www.worthpoint.com/worthopedia/kasparov-galileo-chess-computer-253716395).

