$info=gk2100
$mame
0.268 [hap, Berger]

Kasparov GK 2100, Saitek, 1994

The GK 2100 was released by Saitek in 1993 and contained a program by Frans Morsch, running on an 8-bit H8 processor with 32 KB ROM and 1 KB RAM. It was the successor to the Saitek GK 2000. The flat, discreetly styled black desktop device measures 42 x 28 x 3.7 cm and looks very good. Of course, a chess computer is not just about looks - the features and playing strength must also be right. The GK2100 has a lot to offer its potential owner in these areas. The 32 KByte chess program of the GK2100 was developed by the successful programmer Frans Morsch, who won the absolute world championship title in Hong Kong (see WCCC 1995) with his most famous creation, the PC program Fritz. The program is driven by a fast Hitachi H8 processor clocked at 10 MHz, whose small instruction set allows very fast processing of the chess algorithms and, as a further advantage, has very low energy consumption. The game play exhibits the well-known qualities of all Morsch programs (source, traslated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php/Saitek_GK_2100).

DRIVER:
