$info=gpi1200
$mame
0.275 [Taksangs]

GPi 1200, Goldstar, 1995

The GoldStar GPi 1200 is a portable video game console produced by GoldStar (the predecessor of LG) in the early 2000s. It’s a "plug-and-play" device featuring a built-in LCD display, designed for on-the-go gaming without the need for connection to a TV. The console comes with several pre-installed games, primarily clones or simplified versions of classic arcade titles and retro games, easily accessed via physical controls like a joystick and buttons. Compact and battery-powered, the GPi 1200 stands out for its ergonomic design and straightforward interface, tailored for casual, family-oriented users. Although it wasn’t based on a standard gaming platform, it delivers an instant entertainment experience typical of the affordable mini-consoles popular during that era. Today, it’s regarded as a fascinating example of how Asian electronics manufacturers ventured into the portable retrogaming market before the widespread digital age—and remains a sought-after item among collectors of vintage gadgets and niche gaming consoles.

