$info=grandmas
$mame
0.288 [hap, Berger]

Grand Master (Milton Bradley), Milton Bradley, 1983

Milton Bradley Grand Master portable electronic chess console, released in the 1980s exclusively for chess. Compact design featuring a fixed-segment LCD chessboard, numeric keypad for move entry, and speaker for audio feedback. Based on a 4/8-bit microcontroller, it offers teaching modes for beginners, move analysis, tactical suggestions, and games against the computer with scalable difficulty levels. Includes a physical chessboard with labeled squares and designated areas for captured pieces, as described in the user manual. Battery-powered, with no connectivity or expansion options—dedicated software stored in ROM. Designed for players of all skill levels to facilitate structured learning and autonomous practice, it represents a pioneering example of a pre-digital "chess computer," exclusively focused on chess without features for other board games.

