$info=ht108064
$mame
0.128 [Miodrag Milanović]

HT-1080Z/64, Hiradastechnika Szovetkezet, 1985

The HT-1080Z, HT-2080Z and HT-3080C computers were manufactured by HHiradastechnika Szovetkezet in Budapest, Hungary from 1983 to 1986. The HT-1080Z was one of the first official school computers in Hungary. Initial versions were unaltered VideoGenie I computers relabeled and mounted with an on-board Yamaha/General Instruments AY-3-8910 sound chip. The EACA VideoGenie I was in turn slightly modified clone of the popular TRS-80 Model 1 with Level II BASIC. The Yamaha chip is the same one that appeared in other famous computer models, such as the Amstrad and the Atari ST. HT-1080Z/64 had 48kB RAM and support of accented Hungarian characters (source: http://ht.homeserver.hu/html/english.html).

