$info=husky
$mame
0.213 [Nigel Barnes, Phill Harvey-Smith]

Husky, DVW Microelectronics, 1981

In 1981, responding to a request from Severn Trent Water, DVW designed and manufactured the first rugged handheld computer - the Husky, dramatically featured on the BBC TV programme 'Tomorrow's World'. This caught the attention of the UK Ministry of Defence, who subsequently awarded DVW a contract to supply the MOD with rugged handheld computers as part of the Rapier missile project. Husky (it will become the name of the company some years later) has become since a familiar name in the niche market for rugged field hardware. The Husky was the first, or at least one of the first real portable computer. And as it was waterproof against accidental immersion and rugged, it was very expensive (~5600 Euros). The Husky had a small LCD screen (32 x 4) and a flat membrane keyboard, just like the Sinclair ZX-81 (source: http://www.old-computers.com/museum/computer.asp?st=1&c=471).

