$info=icatel
$mame
0.164 [Felipe Corrêa da Silva Sanches]

TPCI (Brazilian public payphone), Icatel, 1995

The Icatel TPCI was the electronic terminal used in Brazilian public telephone booths starting in the 1990s. Developed by Icatel (a joint venture between Italtel and the Brazilian operator Telebrás), it featured dedicated hardware for handling toll calls, authentication via prepaid cards or coins, and communication with the central network. Designed to withstand harsh environmental conditions and heavy usage, it combined a microcontroller or embedded processor (often based on the Intel 80386SX architecture or a similar one) with a simplified user interface, a numeric keypad, and an alphanumeric display. It was a key component of Brazil’s public telecommunications infrastructure prior to the widespread adoption of mobile phones.

