Plug-and-play TV system produced by Intec/InterAct in the late 2000s—a budget clone of the Nintendo Wii. The system includes a console that connects to televisions via a composite AV jack, two wireless "Genie-Con" controllers equipped with rudimentary motion sensors (similar to the Wiimote but featuring intensely flashing LEDs), one wired classic controller, and plastic accessories such as a golf club and a racket. It comes preloaded with 32 games either built directly into the console or available on additional cartridges—mostly unauthorized clones of sports and action titles. The compact design, powered by batteries in the controllers, is aimed at a young audience seeking instant, low-cost home entertainment.

