InterAct 8-in-1, Intec, 200?

Plug-and-play TV system produced by Intec/InterAct in the late 2000s, a compact version of the Nintendo Wii clone. It connects directly to the TV via a composite AV cable, features a single "Genie-Con" controller with basic motion sensing and a flashing LED, plus inexpensive plastic accessories (racket, bat). The console comes preloaded with 8 games, mostly unauthorized clones of simplified sports and arcade titles. Ultra-low-cost, battery-powered design aimed at children and families seeking instant entertainment at a minimal price—typical of the TV gaming systems widely available at discount stores during that era.

