The Impuls-03 (Импульс-03) is a Soviet 8-bit personal computer introduced in the late 1980s. Unlike most Soviet computers of the time, which were produced in massive state-owned factories, this model was developed and marketed in small quantities by the "Impuls" cooperative to replace the previous, obsolete Impuls-02 model. From an architectural standpoint, the Impuls-03 is a hardware clone of the famous Radio-86RK (Радио-86РК), one of the most iconic DIY and hobbyist computers widely used in the Soviet Union.

