$info=indiana
$mame
0.136 [Miodrag Milanović]

Indiana University 68030 board, Indiana University, 1993

Prototype development board created at Indiana University in the 1980s/1990s, based on the 32-bit Motorola 68030 processor. Designed for academic research and teaching computer architecture, it featured a 68030 CPU with a built-in MMU, static RAM memory ranging from a few megabytes, an expansion bus for custom peripherals, and a serial interface for debugging. Used in university laboratories to experiment with embedded operating systems, compilers, and hardware engineering projects prior to the advent of FPGAs. Documentation was limited, and production was restricted to just a few units intended exclusively for the academic environment, making it an exemplary piece of educational hardware from the pre-Internet era in U.S. universities.

