0.192 [Patrick Mackinlay]

InterPro/InterServe 20x0, Intergraph, 1990

A UNIX workstation produced by Intergraph Corporation in the early 1990s, part of the InterPro series designed for CAD, GIS, and engineering professionals. Based on either a RISC Clipper C100 processor or, in some configurations, an Intel 80486, it ran the CLIX operating system (derived from UNIX System V) or Windows NT. Equipped with proprietary high-performance graphics cards featuring hardware acceleration for 2D/3D applications in technical design. RAM was expandable from 4 MB to over 64 MB, with storage provided by SCSI hard disks and integrated Ethernet connectivity. Featuring a compact tower design with a metal chassis, it was intended for technical studios and industrial environments for solid modeling, architectural rendering, and geospatial analysis prior to the widespread adoption of Intel-based workstations.

