InterPro/InterServe 28x0, Intergraph, 1994

High-end UNIX workstation produced by Intergraph Corporation in the early 1990s, representing the top model in the InterPro series for CAD, GIS, and complex engineering applications. Based on a RISC Clipper C400 processor (or an Intel 80486DX4 in alternative configurations), it ran either the CLIX operating system (UNIX System V Release 4) or Windows NT. Equipped with proprietary high-performance graphics cards featuring advanced 2D/3D hardware acceleration, expandable RAM ranging from 16 MB up to 256 MB, high-capacity SCSI hard disk storage (up to several GB), and integrated Ethernet/FDDI connectivity. It featured a vertical tower design with a robust metal chassis and an optimized cooling system tailored for extended workloads. Aimed at large engineering firms, the aerospace industry, and geospatial analysis, this workstation was used for advanced solid modeling, structural simulations, photorealistic rendering, and scientific visualization—before transitioning to mainstream Intel/AMD platforms in subsequent years.

