InterPro/InterServe 60x0, Intergraph, 1990

An enterprise-class UNIX workstation produced by Intergraph Corporation in the first half of the 1990s, it served as the flagship model in the InterPro series for high-end CAD/CAM/CAE applications and complex engineering simulations. Based on either an RISC MIPS R3000 or R4000 processor (with a multiprocessor option available in advanced configurations), it ran the CLIX operating system (UNIX System V Release 4) augmented with proprietary extensions for advanced graphics management. Equipped with high-performance Intergraph IGX graphics cards featuring dedicated hardware acceleration for 3D rendering, RAM capacity expandable from 32 MB up to 512 MB or more, configurable SCSI RAID arrays for storage, and high-speed Ethernet/FDDI connectivity, this workstation featured a full-size tower design with an industrial-grade metal chassis, redundant power supply, and an advanced cooling system to ensure 24/7 operational reliability. It was targeted at large aerospace companies, automotive manufacturers, and research centers for advanced parametric modeling, computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations, finite element analysis (FEA), and scientific visualization—prior to the transition toward standardized platforms in subsequent years.

