InterPro/InterServe 64x0, Intergraph, 1992

An enterprise-class UNIX workstation produced by Intergraph Corporation in the mid-1990s, this model represented an advanced evolution of the InterPro series, designed for intensive CAD/CAM/CAE applications and scientific visualization. Based on either an Intel Pentium or Pentium Pro processor (with a dual-processor option available in advanced configurations), it marked Intergraph’s strategic transition toward standard x86 architectures while retaining its proprietary IGX graphics cards for dedicated 3D acceleration. The system ran the Windows NT operating system with Intergraph extensions for advanced graphics management or CLIX (UNIX System V) in legacy configurations. It featured expandable RAM ranging from 64 MB up to 1 GB, configurable SCSI RAID arrays for storage, and high-speed Ethernet/FDDI connectivity. The workstation boasted a full-size tower design with an industrial-grade metal chassis, redundant power supply, and an advanced cooling system to ensure continuous operation. It was targeted at large aerospace companies, automotive manufacturers, and government research centers, supporting complex parametric modeling, real-time structural simulations, photorealistic rendering, and geospatial analysis of large datasets—prior to the complete shift toward PC-based platforms in the late 1990s.

