InterPro/InterServe 67x0, Intergraph, 1993

An enterprise-class UNIX workstation produced by Intergraph Corporation in the second half of the 1990s, representing the flagship model in the InterPro series for mission-critical CAD/CAM/CAE applications and advanced scientific visualization. Based on either an Intel Pentium Pro or Pentium II processor (with dual- or quad-processor configurations in high-end versions), it epitomized the pinnacle of integration between the standard x86 architecture and Intergraph’s proprietary graphics technology. It primarily ran Windows NT 4.0 with Intergraph extensions for real-time graphics management, while maintaining legacy support for CLIX (UNIX System V) in established environments. Equipped with IGX-Extreme graphics cards featuring dedicated 3D hardware acceleration and dedicated video memory, it offered expandable RAM ranging from 128 MB up to 2 GB, configurable hot-swappable SCSI RAID arrays for storage, and Gigabit Ethernet/FDDI connectivity. The workstation featured a full-size tower design with an industrial-grade metal chassis certified for harsh environments, redundant hot-swappable power supplies, and a modular cooling system ensuring 24/7/365 operational availability. Targeted at major aerospace companies (Boeing, Lockheed Martin), premium automotive manufacturers, and government laboratories, this workstation was used for complex parametric modeling, real-time structural simulations, virtual crash testing, offline photorealistic rendering, and geospatial analysis of terrestrial and satellite datasets—prior to the eventual shift toward standardized PC-based workstations by the end of the decade.

