0.198 [Patrick Mackinlay]

InterPro/InterServe 68x0, Intergraph, 1993

An enterprise-class UNIX workstation produced by Intergraph Corporation in the late 1990s/early 2000s, representing the top-of-the-line model in the InterPro series for mission-critical CAD/CAM/CAE applications and high-performance scientific visualization. Based on Intel Pentium II/III or Pentium III Xeon processors (with quad-processor or octo-processor configurations in high-end versions), it marked the final evolution of the integration between the standard x86 architecture and Intergraph’s proprietary graphics technology prior to the company’s acquisition. It primarily ran Windows NT 4.0 and Windows 2000 with Intergraph extensions for real-time graphics management, while maintaining legacy support for CLIX (UNIX System V) in established environments. Equipped with IGX-Extreme II graphics cards featuring dedicated 3D hardware acceleration, advanced framebuffer memory, and support for simultaneous multi-display setups, it offered expandable RAM from 256 MB up to 4 GB or more, configurable hot-swappable Ultra160 SCSI RAID arrays for storage, and full-duplex Gigabit Ethernet connectivity. The workstation featured a full-size tower design with an industrial-grade metal chassis certified for mission-critical environments, N+1 redundant hot-swappable power supply, and a laminar-flow cooling system ensuring continuous 24/7/365 operation. It was targeted at major aerospace companies (Boeing, Lockheed Martin, Northrop Grumman), the global automotive industry, and government agencies (NASA, DoD) for highly complex parametric modeling, real-time structural and fluid-dynamic simulations, high-fidelity virtual crash testing, distributed photorealistic rendering, and geospatial analysis of multi-terabyte satellite datasets—representing the pinnacle of Intergraph’s proprietary workstations before the company’s eventual transition to standardized PC platforms and its acquisition by Hexagon AB in the early 2000s.

