$info=ipb
$mame
0.136 [Robbbert]

iPB, Intel, 19??

A hybrid device released by Intel in 1994, also known as the Intel Personal Communicator (models 320/325), combined the functionalities of a GSM mobile phone, a handheld PDA, and a fax modem into a single device. It was based on a 20-MHz Intel 386EX processor with 2 MB of RAM and featured a 4.5-inch monochrome touch-screen LCD display. The device ran a proprietary operating system with a graphical user interface for managing contacts, calendars, notes, and voice/data communications. It included a PCMCIA slot for memory expansion and connectivity, an integrated numeric keypad, and an external antenna for cellular network access. Powered by a rechargeable battery, it represented one of the earliest attempts at converging mobile telephony and handheld computing prior to the advent of modern smartphones. Primarily marketed in limited quantities in Europe and Asia, it has become a collector’s item due to its pioneering nature in integrating portable communication and productivity features.

