0.136 [Robbbert]

iPC, Intel, 19??

A hybrid device released by Intel in 1994, commercially known as the Intel Personal Communicator (abbreviated as iPC, models 320/325), combined the functionalities of a GSM mobile phone, a PDA handheld computer, and a fax modem into a single device. It was based on an Intel 386EX processor running at 20 MHz, equipped with 2 MB of RAM and a 4.5-inch monochrome touch-screen LCD display. The device ran a proprietary operating system featuring a graphical user interface for managing contacts, calendars, notes, and voice/data communications. It included a PCMCIA slot for memory expansion and connectivity, a built-in numeric keypad, and an external antenna for the GSM 900 cellular network. Powered by a rechargeable lithium-ion battery, it represented one of the earliest attempts at converging mobile telephony and handheld computing prior to the advent of modern smartphones. Primarily marketed in limited quantities in Europe and Asia (estimated at fewer than 10,000 units), it quickly became a collector's item due to its pioneering nature in integrating mobile communication and portable productivity in the pre-smartphone era.

