iSBC 80/30, Intel, 1978

Single-board computer Intel based on the 8080/8085 processor (8-bit), produced in the late 1970s/early 1980s for embedded and industrial applications. It integrated a CPU, RAM/ROM memory, controllers for serial/parallel I/O, and an expansion slot all on a single compact board conforming to the Multibus standard. Widely used in automation, instrumentation, control systems, and as a development platform for embedded software. It supported operating systems such as ISIS (Intel System Implementation Supervisor) and CP/M. Featuring a rugged design suited for industrial environments, it included standard connectors for rack integration. This device represented a modular "all-in-one" solution prior to the emergence of standardized industrial PCs—a typical example of the Intel Multibus ecosystem for 8-bit embedded systems.

