$info=iskr3104
$mame
0.143u3 [temaperm, anasana]

Iskra 3104, Schetmash, 1992

Soviet/Russian PC produced by the Schetmash plant in Kursk in 1992. An upgraded version of the Iskra 1031, it was based on the KR1810VM86 processor (a Soviet clone of the 16-bit Intel 8086) and was compatible with the IBM PC/XT architecture. It featured EGA video support with Cyrillic character set capabilities for Russian-language display, expandable RAM, and ISA expansion slots. Intended for professional, educational, and industrial use in the post-Soviet era, it represented an attempt to modernize Russian home computing before the widespread adoption of Western PCs. It was marketed in limited quantities due to the collapse of the Soviet electronics industry and the rapid proliferation of Western PC clones during the 1990s.

