0.130 [Juergen Buchmueller, Frank Palazzolo, Sean Riddle]

ITT Tele-Match Processor, ITT, 197?

ITT Tele-Match Processor it's a clone of the Fairchild Channel F is a game console released by Fairchild Semiconductor in August 1976 at the retail price of $169.95. It has the distinction of being the first programmable ROM cartridge-based video game console, and the first console to use a microprocessor. It was launched as the Video Entertainment System, or VES, but when Atari released their VCS the next year, Fairchild renamed its machine. By 1977, the Fairchild Channel F had sold 250,000 units and was second-place behind the VCS (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairchild_Channel_F).

