$info=ivant
$mame
0.268 [hap, Sean Riddle]

Ivan The Terrible (H8/3216 version), Excalibur Electronics, 1997

Ivan The Terrible, (Excalibur 701E Ivan The Terrible) a talking dedicated chess computer by Excalibur Electronics, released in 1996. The computer has a Hitachi H8/3216 controller running at 12 MHz with 48 KiB ROM and 2 KiB RAM, and features a press sensory board along with LCD, button panel and speaker. The program was Ron Nelson's further development of his H8 program of the Excalibur Mirage applying a tactical quiescence search due to attack maps, adding sound effects and speech synthesis with his own compression algorithm. Evaluation tuning was supported by chess consultant Larry Kaufman, who was also responsible for the opening book (source: https://www.chessprogramming.org/Ivan_The_Terrible).

