$info=jd368
$mame
0.273 [QUFB]

Super Picky Talk - Access Pet, Casio, 1997?

An electronic device for children produced by Casio in the early 1990s, known as "Super Picky Talk" in Japan and "Access Pet" in certain Western markets. This interactive virtual pet featured speech synthesis that emitted phrases and sounds in response to the user’s caresses, thanks to tactile sensors on its surface. It was equipped with a monochromatic LCD screen displaying the virtual animal—a humanoid character with large eyes—and required periodic attention (food, play, cleaning) to keep the "pet" happy. Powered by batteries, it had a compact design made of colorful plastic and included buttons for interacting with the device. As a conceptual predecessor to the Tamagotchi, it represented an early experiment in interactive toy electronics with vocal feedback, preceding the era of mass-market virtual pets.

