$info=jtc
$mame
0.133 [Curt Coder]

CompJU+TEr, Jugend+Technik, 1987

Jugend+Technik was a magazine for young people in the German Democratic Republic (GDR), which was dedicated to the topics of technology and popular science. It was published by the publishing world and boy of the Free German Youth (FDJ). In the editions from 1987 until the cessation of the journal as a guide for making your own computer Ju-Te computer was released. In each issue, a part of the computer has been explained, printed circuit diagrams and code offered parts of the operating system, as well as small applications for typing. Published after completion of the basic system Youth + Technology regular extensions for Ju-Te computer, for example, an EPROM programmer module, memory upgrades, and interfaces for connecting various peripherals. Software for these and other computer GDR production was later offered in collaboration with the Youth Radio DT64, via radio frequencies for call recording on cassette. Due to the often poor availability of parts, the construction of this computer was for many readers just theory (source, traslated from german: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jugend_und_Technik).

