$info=jupace
$mame
0.36b12 [Paul Daniels]

Jupiter Ace, Jupiter Cantab, 1981

The Jupiter Ace was a British home computer of the early 1980s, produced by a company, set up for the purpose, named Jupiter Cantab. The Ace differed from other microcomputers of the time in that it used FORTH instead of the more common BASIC. The Jupiter Ace system was adapted to the disk-less tape-using home computer hardware. On average, and for similar programs, ACE's FORTH was 5 times faster and used half the memory (which was a costly luxury at the time) of an equivalent program written in interpreted BASIC. Forth programs tend to be more memory efficient than the bigger programs; as they become bigger, they reuse more previously-defined code (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jupiter_Ace).

