$info=kblitz
$mame
0.269 [hap, Berger]

Kasparov Blitz (set 1), Saitek / Heuristic Software, 1990

In 1990, Julio Kaplan really broke new ground in every respect with Kasparov Blitz: A fast program, but one with far less chess knowledge, offers better solution times in tactical positions in direct comparison with its bigger brothers. As the name suggests, a suitable training partner should be created for blitz and rapid chess games in particular. The Blitz differs from the other Saitek products not only in terms of playing style: Namely, it also includes a technology called "scan logic operation", which controls all necessary functions with two dials, without buttons or knobs (except for the discreetly concealed on/off switch). Saitek also equipped the flash with a completely new type of sensor system that neither reacts to pressure nor works magnetically. This made it possible for this device to be suitable for lightning and high-speed chess. The 43 x 33 x 4 cm chess computer (playing area 25 x 25 cm) contains a 32KB program running on a 10 MHz H8 processor. In addition to 64 game, analysis and problem levels and an eleven-digit display with a wide range of display options, the Blitz also has a position memory. The opening library contains around 17,000 half-moves (according to the BDA only 6,000). Pure hobby and occasional players are certainly well served with the Kasparov Blitz (source, translated from German:https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php/Saitek_Kasparov_Blitz).

DRIVER:
saitek/blitz.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap and Title.

WIP:
- 0.269: hap and Berger added 'Saitek / Heuristic Software Kasparov Blitz (set 1)' system.

ROMSET:
127480 bytes in 2 files / 41.93Kb packed

$end
