$end
$info=kc85_2
$mame
0.132 [Kevin Thacker, Sandro Ronco]

HC900 / KC 85/2, VEB Mikroelektronik "Wilhelm Pieck" Mühlhausen, 1987

It was based on the 8-bit CPU U880 (a Z80 clone) with 1.75 MHz (HC 900 = KC 85/2, KC 85/3) or 1.773MHz (KC 85/4) CPU clock. The typical use of Mulhouse computer was a KC 85/3 with 32 Kbytes of RAM (expandable with additional modules), 16 Kbytes of ROM BASIC, a connected tape recorder for data storage and connection to a used as a monitor TV (on UHF modulator, composite video output or RGB output). The KC 85/2, he had only 4 Kbytes ROM, ROM BASIC and lacked the lowercase letters. The KC 85/4 came with 128 KB RAM and better graphics capabilities, but they were exploited by the collapse of the GDR hardly. All KC 85 from Mühlhausen were graphics capability, and the screen resolution was 320x256 pixels, however, was the "color resolution" much less, in a pixel rectangle 4x8 pixels, it was only a foreground color (from a possible 16) and a background color (from 8 possible ) indicate, this restriction decreased the KC 85/4 on a line of 1x8 pixels and also has a "real" color mode with 4 colors and no limit can be turned on. Only at the KC 85/4 eliminated the annoying peculiarity of Muhlhauser KC-row that caused the CPU memory accesses to the memory screen image distortion (source, traslated from german: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleincomputer_KC_85/2-4#HC_900_und_KC_85.2F2).

