$info=kminus
$mame
0.232 [Nigel Barnes, Alessandro Liberalato]

Kyber Minus, Kyber Calcolatori, 1982

In the 1980s, several companies launched into the business computer market, mainly offering office CP/M machines. In Tuscany the main ones were the General Processor of Florence and Kyber Calcolatori of Pistoia, in particular the latter produced several computers, among which we find the Kyber Minus. The Kyber Minus was essentially sold in two versions: the first with the card, the power supply and two 5 "25 floppies in a wooden case with an external video terminal; the second is in fact the only "Big Board" fixed on a wooden table with the power supply and 2 floppy drives, typically 8-inch, but the user could also purchase it with a pair of 5 "25 drives (source, traslated from Italian: https://www.apuliaretrocomputing.it/wordpress/?p=8777).

