$info=krokha
$mame
0.210 [Sergey Svishchev]

Krokha, SKB Kontur, 1990

The computer layout is similar to the "Specialist" computer. The main difference from the computer "Specialist" is that in "Krokh" they implemented a text video adapter, not a graphic one. Screen size 48x32 characters. All characters are black and white. The character generator contains 256 characters. The ROM of the character generator is installed on a replaceable printed circuit board, a cartridge. The use of two removable ROMs, for the program and for graphics (characters), is very similar to the prefix Dendy (Nintendo Entertainment System). Only one cartridge was produced for the computer. It contained games: Wall, Tetris, Caterpillar, Balls, Air Defense. In Krokh, the video frame sync is connected to a processor interrupt. A static memory is used, the information in which is stored after the power is turned off thanks to a galvanic cell. There are only 2048 bytes of RAM, of which 1536 bytes are used by video, and only 512 bytes remain for the programmer. From the author of the computer, caro: There was a period in my life when I worked at SKB Kontur. That's when I developed this prefix called "Baby". We then sat on the territory of the Technosvyaz plant, which was supposed to assemble these cars after conversion. But they made about 200 pieces and they did not get into the stores - they went to the workers of the plant and the SKB Kontur. The main problem of course - the lack of games (source: https://alemorf.github.io/retro_computers/computer.html?id=Kroha).

DRIVER:
ussr/krokha.cpp

