0.200 [R. Belmont, Peter Ferrie, KansasFest 2018]

Laser //c, Milmar, 1985

Computer home Milmar Laser //c (or Laser IIc)—an unauthorized Brazilian clone of the Apple II, produced by Milmar in the 1980s during a period of import restrictions on computers in Brazil. Despite its name and compact case inspired by the Apple IIc, it was technically a clone of the Apple II Plus, featuring ROMs that were identical to the originals from Apple for maximum software compatibility. Based on a MOS 6502/65C02 processor running at 1 MHz with 64–128 KB of RAM, it included limited expansion slots compared to the original Apple II but retained ports for joysticks, composite video output, and support for 5¼-inch floppy disk drives. The compact design featured beige plastic casing with an integrated handle on the top cover, resembling the aesthetic of the Apple IIc. Intended for the Brazilian educational and home markets as an affordable alternative to the original Apple computers, it stands as a prime example of Brazil’s computer cloning industry during that era—often produced without official licenses and featuring the Apple logo used improperly.

DRIVER:
apple/apple2.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
apple2_flop_clcracked.xml, apple2_flop_orig.xml, apple2_flop_misc.xml, apple2_cass.xml

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon and machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection).

