$info=leonardoa
$mame
0.221 [hap, Berger]

Kasparov Leonardo (v1.2), SciSys / Heuristic Software, 1986

Chess computer manufactured by Scisys (Hong Kong). In just a few years (since its separation from Novag in 1979) the company had established itself as the largest manufacturer of chess computers worldwide (it developed more than 50 different models from 1980 to 1986) reaching sales in a single year (1986 ) more than 100,000 units. That same year, it delegated the manufacture of the computers to its subsidiary "Saitek". The company had been officially endorsed by the chess player Kasparov since 1983. When the player won the world championship in Moscow in 1985, all the company's computers began to bear the name of the new world champion. The Astral (1985) with a size of 31x31 cm was the first in a series of Scisys computers with a wooden frame and a sensory (snap-on) plastic board, followed by the Leonardo (1986) with a size of 52x52 cm, made of wood with a magnetic sensory board (38x38 cm) and the Stratos (1987), now by Saitek, with an intermediate size (39 cm on each side) and external appearance and quality similar to the Astral. The Astral had its continuation with the Virtuoso (1992) and the Capella (1998); the Leonardo with the Galileo (1988) and the Renaissance (1989), while the Stratos had only one successor, the Corona (1988). The Leonardo had a modular design. Its OSA (Open Systems Architecture) architecture allowed software and hardware expansions. It came to have three expansion modules available: Saitek Analyst, Saitek Maestro and Saitek Brute Force. It also had a serial port to connect to a compatible computer (source, traslated from Spanish: https://retroordenadoresorty.blogspot.com/2020/03/scisys-leonardo-kasparov-1986.html).

DRIVER:
saitek/leonardo.cpp

