$info=ls5amvp3
$mame
0.286 [The Retro Web]

5AMVP3 (VIA MVP3 chipset), Lucky Star, 1998

Cost-effective OEM motherboard produced by Lucky Star (an Asian brand specializing in PC clones), based on the VIA MVP3 chipset (VT82C505), widely used from 1998 to 1999. Designed for Socket 7/Super Socket 7 processors: AMD K6-2, K6-III, and Intel Pentium II/MMX. Typical features include mixed ISA/PCI slots, support for SDRAM up to 768 MB–1 GB, a built-in IDE controller with two ATA-33/66 channels, optional AC'97 audio, and serial/parallel ports. The MVP3 chipset offered decent 3D performance for its time thanks to the AGP 2x interface, though it had limitations in managing external L2 cache memory. Typical of entry-level PC builds and Asian clones from the Windows 98/ME era, this motherboard represented an affordable solution prior to the dominance of official Intel chipsets. It did not include advanced features such as robust overclocking or multiprocessor support.

DRIVER:
pc/pcipc_via.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Preliminary
Color: Good
Sound: Imperfect
Graphics: Imperfect
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
