$info=m3
$mame
0.192 [Robbbert]

M-THREE, LSI, 1982

LSI is a British manufacturer based in Working, attached to McLaren, but it has nothing to do with Alonso's car. The company targeted business applications such as word processing, databases, and spreadsheets. Just what Sir Clive had thought. It was also geared as a CPU for instrumentation systems. Comment that this orientation as an instrumentation team had many teams of the time, such as the Rockwell AIM 65. Initially, the M-One was for business, with proprietary software. Then came the M-Two, already multi-user, but also limited to LSI soft. LSI did not support other CP/M-type systems, because it was less efficient than its own OS. With the M-Three, based on a Z80, it became a general purpose computer. Its launch lasted two and a half years selling a whopping 3000 units, being later succeeded by the M-Four, which came with the Z80B, double the speed of a normal Z80. As well as equipped with an 8088 in turn (source, traslated from Spanish: http://rwiki.retrowiki.es/pages/revision/1731).

