$info=maciisi
$mame
0.135 [R. Belmont]

Macintosh IIsi, Apple Computer, 1990

The Macintosh IIsi was a compact three-box desktop unit, effectively a cut-down Macintosh IIci in a smaller case (used for no other Macintosh model), made cheaper by the redesign of the motherboard and the deletion of all but one of the expansion card slots (a single Processor Direct Slot). It was introduced as a low-cost alternative to the professional desktop models for home use, but offered more features and performance than the LC series. It had color and could drive a number of different external monitors, with a maximum screen resolution of 640x480 in eight-bit color (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macintosh_IIsi).

