Macintosh Plus (Japan), Apple Computer, 1986

Iconic 1980s desktop computer, belonging to the first generation of the revolutionary line that introduced the graphical user interface and the mouse to the general public. Equipped with a Motorola 68000 processor, 1 MB of RAM (expandable), a 3.5-inch floppy drive, and an operating system featuring an integrated GUI. Its compact, minimalist design includes a built-in monochromatic display, making it ideal for writing, graphics, and personal productivity tasks. The system supports external peripherals and dedicated software, playing a pivotal role in popularizing computer use in creative and home environments. The Japanese version comes with a localized keyboard and software adaptations tailored specifically for the local market.

