$info=mako
$mame
0.288 [Nigel Barnes]

Diamond Mako, SONICblue, 2000

Handheld PDA manufactured by SONICblue (formerly S3) around the year 2000, this was the U.S. version of the well-known Psion Revo+. It features a compact design with a metal casing, a 4.8-inch monochrome LCD display (480×160), a built-in QWERTY keyboard, and a stylus for touchscreen input. Powered by a 32-bit ARM710T processor, it comes with 8 MB of RAM and 16 MB of ROM, running the EPOC32 operating system (a precursor to Symbian). The device includes infrared connectivity, a docking station for synchronization with a PC, and a rechargeable battery. It was aimed at professionals and tech-savvy users as an advanced personal organizer—not a general-purpose consumer device. This PDA represents the final era of pre-smartphone PDAs, renowned for its efficiency, long battery life, and rugged durability.

