$info=max80
$mame
0.231 [Robbbert]

MAX-80, Lobo Systems, 1982

The MAX-80 small business computer has exceptional expansion capabilities. Built into this single keyboard unit are controllers for flexible disk drives, both 5-inch and 8-inch, interfaces for flexible and fixed hard disk drives, and several communication ports. Nearly all of the software that runs on the Radio Shack TRS-80 Model I and Model III, LDOS will run on the MAX-80 and visa versa. The MAX-80 is a disk system and requires a disk drive for operation. It supports two popular disk operating systems - LDOS and CP/M. When the machine is booted, that operating system is loaded into the memory and executed. Turning the machine ON without a disk-based operating system greets you with continuous 'beeps' and nothing else. What is LDOS? LDOS is an operating system written by Logical Systems Inc. to run on the TRS-80 Model I computer. Logical Systems was actually spun-off from Lobo Systems, so they could market their new and powerful Operating Systems to others. Radio Shack liked LDOS so much, that it became the official hard disk operating system for the TRS-80 Model III (TRS-DOS 6.0) (source: http://oldcomputers.net/lobomax80.html).

