$info=mbc55x
$mame
0.142 [Phill Harvey-Smith]

MBC-55x, Sanyo, 1983

The Sanyo MBC-550 was the first of the legitimate "clones" of the IBM Personal Computer. While others (notably the Taiwanese) were duplicating the circuitry and Read-Only Memories (ROMs) of the IBM PC, Sanyo Business Systems designed their own circuitry and wrote their own Basic Input/Output System (BIOS), part of which was in ROM and part was on disk. The character set was also in ROM. The Sanyo 55x was the first MS-DOS compatible computer that retailed for under US$1000. It was also quite possibly the only (somewhat) IBM-compatible system that was actually slower than an IBM PC (the PC clocked at 4.77 MHz, while the Sanyo clocked at 3.6, giving it a Norton SI rating of 0.8) (source: http://www.old-computers.com/museum/computer.asp?st=1&c=473).

DRIVER:
sanyo/mbc55x.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
mbc55x.xml

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon, machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection), Control Panel, Flyer and Marquee.

WIP:
- 0.247: Remove some residual debugging stuff [AJR].
- 0.206: Re-implemented high-level emulation of keyboard as a serial device [AJR].
- 0.205: Added 8087 coprocessor [AJR].
- 0.204: BugFIX #06718 [Crash/Freeze] (mbc55x.cpp) mbc55x: Basic freezes after syntax error [AJR]. Additions and refinements: Corrected all clocks, and derived them from the master crystal. Corrected I/O address decoding. Connected speaker output to 8251 USART. Add ISA expansion slot, Centronics printer port, and RS-232 "line" port. Added backspace key to HLE keyboard and noted undumped microcontroller [AJR].
- 0.148u5: Added a few more disks at softlist [pete_j].
- 0.142: Phill Harvey-Smith added 'Sanyo MBC-55x' system.

ROMSET:
8192 bytes in 1 file / 2.01Kb packed

$end
