$info=mbeeic
$mame
0.124 [Robbbert]

Microbee 32k IC, Applied Technology, 1983

The Microbee 64K was available as a 3rd choice alongside the 16K and 32K machines and was aimed more at the business market. This machine is normally configured to boot CP/M from disk and use floppy disks for program storage rather than booting to Basic and using tapes like the 16K/32K machines. The master and video dot clocks are changed from 12MHz to 13.5Mhz. The font ROM has been expanded from 2KB to 4KB to support dual fonts required for the dual display resolutions. The last two items will later be applied to the 16K and 32K models as well as part of the IC release (source:https://www.microbee-mspp.org.au/wiki/tiki-index.php?page=Microbee+Series+1+Models).

