$info=mbh1
$mame
0.250 [openMSX]

MB-H1 (MSX1, Japan), Hitachi, 1983

The Hitachi MB-H1 was aimed at the Japanese market. This model was released in two colors, "Elegant Almond" (EA) at first then also "Trad Red" (TR). It has a CPU speed controller switch on the right side, allowing slow games execution. The main unit is equipped with a pull-out handle under the keyboard, and the power supply can be attached to the main unit for transport. It was marketed as being easy to take with you having about an A4 size. In marketing literature they would show it being carried, typically by a school child or in a briefcase without the power supply, which can give you the impression the large power brick is optional, which it is not. The power supply is quite heavy. The computer can be used with power supply attached to the unit, but you can not access the printer, analog sound or composite video ports as well. The power connection from the power supply to the MSX is via a cable which plugs into the side of the MSX (source: https://www.msx.org/wiki/Hitachi_MB-H1).

