$info=mekd5
$mame
0.214 [68bit]

MEK6802D5, Motorola, 1980

Development board for the Motorola MC6802 microprocessor, introduced in the early 1980s as an evolution of the MEK6800D4 series. Features the MC6802 CPU with 128 bytes of internal RAM and a 1 MHz clock, enhanced monitor ROM with improved debugging capabilities, parallel and serial I/O ports, bus expansion connectors, and external power input. Designed for engineers and hobbyists to study the 6800 architecture, develop firmware, and prototype embedded applications. Operated with a serial keyboard and video terminal; programming is done in assembly via the onboard monitor. Represents an upgraded educational platform in the pre-microcontroller era for systems based on the Motorola 6800 family, offering refined tools over the D4 variant.

