The Microcomputer for Education (MFA) is a German microcomputer for training. He was, For example, in the field of communication electronics. The MFA is modular in a 19 "housing and can be equipped with a wide range of expansion cards, all components, including the basic components, such as CPU, RAM or ROM, are designed as plug-in cards to the data, address and system bus of the' system.The MFA is thus well suited to the processes in a computer.Planning is usually done in assembler and can be connected to a PC via the RS232 interface on which a terminal emulation, which can then be used to add the keyboard inputs and the screen output, but it also has its own keyboard and monitor, which was later added 'to the CP / M operating system, using a bootstrap loader even copied into the memory and then turned off the EPROM to load the first sector of the floppy disk, from which further routines for loading the operating system CP / M were found. After shutting down the EPROM, up to 64KB of RAM was available, which could use CP / M. Communication with the operator then ran through an 8251 UART card in conjunction with a terminal. The MAT85 system was used to communicate via the SID and SOD pins of the 8085 processor. The serial protocol was only implemented by software. When the 8251-UART is used against the software solution, a lot of computing power is freed, in which other tasks can be taken over by the processor (source, traslated from German: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikrocomputer_f%C3%BCr_Ausbildung).

