$info=microtrol
$mame
0.201 [MCbx]

unknown Microtrol portable computer, Microtrol, 1985

Not much is known about this 8-bit portable microcomputer. It's a device with built-in green CRT monitor and keyboard. Presence of ROM (?EPROM?) programming socket reminds old industrial machines. As primary storage a 3.5" floppy disk is used by built-in drive. Today the manufacturer is still in market selling industrial automation systems, but not much is known about this computer. The machine is made of parts existing in market in early 1980s. Keyboard is a Cherry terminal keyboard, monitor is also a component, power supply comes from some industrial' system. The CPU is implemented on two Double-Euro sized boards. First one is a 48kB German (West Germany) computer called Eltec Eurocom 2 (v. 5, sometimes mentioned as  "Bystronic"), this computer was used in early 1980s in industrial applications and for driving sound recording/sampling/synthesizing equipment. Second board contains floppy disk drive controller, additional S-RAM and secondary RAM memory. Secondary RAM memory is unusually large for such computer (512kB) so I think that it's a RAM disk for storing some data. S-RAM memory may be used as a buffer for some on-board controllers (source: http://oldcomputer.info/8bit/microtrol/index.htm).

DRIVER:
skeleton/eurocom2.cpp

