0.190 [rfka01, R. Belmont]

Miniframe, Convergent, 1985

Convergent Miniframe computer system, a professional workstation based on the Intel 80186/80286 processor, manufactured by Convergent Technologies in the early 1980s. It features a proprietary architecture with the CTOS (Convergent Technologies Operating System), designed specifically for enterprise environments and local area networks. The system includes an integrated monochromatic high-resolution display, a dedicated keyboard, an optional floppy disk drive, and a hard disk. It supports cooperative multitasking and resource sharing over a network via a proprietary protocol. Used in banks, government offices, and data centers as an intelligent terminal or lightweight server prior to the widespread adoption of PC and Unix standards, the Convergent Miniframe represents an example of pre-PC integrated computing focused on reliability, security, and centralized management.

