$info=mmf9000
$mame
0.128 [Curt Coder]

MicroMainFrame 9000, Commodore Business Machines, 1981

The Commodore PET (Personal Electronic Transactor) was a home/personal computer produced in 1977 by Commodore International. A top-seller in the Canadian and United States educational markets, it was Commodore's first full-featured computer, and formed the basis for their entire 8-bit product line. CPU: MOS 6502 and Motorola 6809, 1 MHz. RAM: 96 kB. ROM: 48 kB, including BASIC 4.0 and other programming languages (Waterloo MicroAPL, MicroFORTRAN, MicroBASIC, MicroPascal, MicroCOBOL). Video: MOS 6545, 12" monochrome monitor, 80x25 character display. Sound: single piezo "beeper" (optional external speaker driven by MOS 6522 CB2 pin)
Ports: MOS 6520 PIA, MOS 6522 VIA, MOS 6551 ACIA, 1 RS-232, 2 Datassette ports (1 on the back), 1 IEEE-488. Notes: basically an 8000 with ROMs for programming languages, it also had three character sets, and an RS-232 for use as a terminal (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commodore_PET#SuperPET_9000_series).

