0.147u4 [Any, The Dumping Union]

Modello T, General Processor, 1979

From 1977 onwards we are witnessing the birth of hundreds of companies that want to produce microcircuits. Most of those born in the U.S. or Japan, but in Europe there are many initiatives. In Italy the old Olivetti contributes enough, although always keeps a cut side calculators and other office machines writers. Even Florence, with the scientific contribution of the University, does its part. In particular, it was assumed that the GP machine more 'easy to produce and was able to run programs evolved and, in particular, programs written with Microsoft Extended Basic of the newborn (Figure Paul Allen and Bill Gates at the presentation of their language). The experiment showed the usefulness CHILD Z 'on the Basic machines of this type. In addition, it was now also developed the ability 'to control peripherals such as keyboards, video and memories floppy. Ultimately, the GP was ripe for the design of a computer complete with all peripherals. For the new machine was chosen name Model T. The name wanted to call the first mass-produced car from Ford, but it was also taken up by T of television, from which he copied some modular techniques of mass production. A problem of GP was the lack of instrumentation for testing of prototypes and testing post-production. The construction techniques available on the market were not sufficiently reliable for an industrial production also small (source, traslated from italian: http://telemat.det.unifi.it/Home_pages/fpirri/Storia/PrimoPC.html#inizio).

