$info=montreal
$mame
0.259 [hap, anonymous]

Mephisto Montreal 68000, Hegener + Glaser, 1993

With the Mephisto Montreal 68000, Hegener & Glaser brought a device made of precious wood onto the market in 1993 that, in addition to being easy to use, had a high level of playing ability at an affordable price. The device measures 38.5 cm x 38.5 cm x 4 cm (king height 8 cm) and looks very elegant. The control unit is located on the front of the game board and can be completely pushed into the device if necessary. The game is very pleasant on the computer, which is equipped with magnetic sensors and individual field LEDs, and allows the figures to be moved naturally. All features and functions could be selected using the keyboard on the front, but the operation was very complicated and not in line with the standards of the time. The computer communicated the information to its opponent via the well-known 4-digit 7-Segemnet display, already known from the MM IV, which of course did not offer such precise display options as, for example, the Polgar with its 32-digit dot matrix display. The chess engine was the old world championship program of Mephisto Roma by Richard Lang from 1987 (source, traslated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php/Mephisto_Montreal_68000).

DRIVER:
hegenerglaser/roma2.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title and Flyer.

WIP:
- 0.259: hap and anonymous added 'Hegener + Glaser Mephisto Montreal 68000' system.

