0.288 [R. Belmont, DBWBP]

MPC60 MIDI Production Center (with SCSI), Akai Electric, 1988

The Akai MPC60, a professional sampler and sequencer launched in 1988 as the first model in the MPC series, features a rack-mount design with a 16-pressure-sensitive pads keyboard, a monochromatic LCD display, and a full MIDI interface. Based on the Motorola 68000 processor, it includes 750 KB of sample memory (expandable), a 12-bit AD/DA converter, and floppy disk drive support for loading and saving samples. The version equipped with SCSI adds connectivity to external hard drives for advanced sample management. Aimed at professional studios and hip-hop/electronic music producers, the MPC60 was revolutionary for its “all-in-one” workflow, seamlessly integrating sampling, sequencing, and mixing. It stands as a milestone in pre-DAW digital music production, emphasizing tactile creativity and hardware autonomy without reliance on external computers.

DRIVER:
akai/mpc60.cpp

