$info=mt65
$mame
0.37b5 [Juergen Buchmueller]

Microtan 65, Tangerine, 1979

The Tangerine Microtan 65 (sometimes abbreviated M65) was a 6502 based single board microcomputer, first sold in 1979, which could be expanded into, what was for its day, a comprehensive and powerful' system. The design became the basis for what later became the ORIC, ATMOS and later computers, which had similar keyboard addressing and tape I/O as in the Microtan 65. The Microtan 65 had a single step function that could be used for debugging at the hardware level. The computer was available as ready-built boards or as kits consisting of board and components requiring soldering together (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangerine_Microtan_65).

