$info=nmicro
$mame
0.223 [hap, Berger, Achim]

Micro Chess, Novag Industries, 1981

Novag Micro Chess, one of Novag's first dedicated chess computer with a program by David Kittinger, first released in 1981. The computer has a Mostek MK3875/42 microcontroller, a second sourced single chip implementation of the Fairchild F8 multi-chip processor, the 3850 ALU with 64 byte scratchpad RAM, which are 64 general purpose 8-bit registers, and the 3851 program storage unit, instruction decoder and 4 Kibibyte mask-programmable ROM, and 64 byte executable RAM. The novelty of Novag Micro Chess was a sensor pegboard with membrane switches, which allows entering moves directly by pulling and sticking pieces on the board. Eight rank and file LEDs indicate origin- and target square of the move made internally by the computer (source: https://www.chessprogramming.org/Novag_Micro_Chess).

DRIVER:
novag/micro.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon and machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection).

WIP:
- 0.264: Changed manufacturer to 'Novag Industries'.
- 0.223: hap, Berger and Achim added 'Novag Micro Chess' system.

ROMSET:
4096 bytes in 1 file / 3.22Kb packed

$end
$info=nmicro2
$mame
0.185 [hap, Berger]

Micro II (Novag), Novag Industries / Heuristic Software, 1982

This device from Hong Kong was the improved version of Micro Chess, which was also a simple and inexpensive chess computer for beginners, which was characterized above all by its ease of use. Train input is via pressure sensors located under the holes of the integrated breadboard. The connectors also hold the figures in place when the board is turned upside down. So you can also play a game in bed, lying on your back. The device was intended for mains-independent operation, but the six alkaline batteries only sufficed for about 20 hours of gaming fun. In the long run, gaming was expensive, which justified the purchase of a power supply. The train is issued with two times eight diodes on the edge of the game board. Other LEDs indicate the color and the different operating modes. The device has eight game levels, one of which is designed as an analysis level and one as a tournament level. Eight clearly laid out buttons control playing strength, position checks, corrections, changing sides, taking back up to two half moves, the acoustic signals that can be switched off, as well as deleting and entering. If it's your turn, you should switch to "Memory". This saves batteries and still saves the position for about a week. This doesn't bother the Micro II, since it doesn't have Permanent Brain anyway. As a rule, he is satisfied with three to four half-moves in the tournament level and eight half-moves in the analysis level, for which he then needs many hours (source, traslated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php/Novag_Micro_II).

DRIVER:
novag/micro2.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

