The Philips NMS 9100 PC (also known as Philips P3105) was a personal computer with an Intel 8088-2 microprocessor and selectable frequency between 4.77 and 8.0 MHz by MS DOS command. Coming on sale in September [1979]], it was supplied as standard with a 5 "internal 360KB Floppy Disk Drive, plus an internal 20MB Hard Disk. The basic graphics card was the four-color CGA (Color Graphics Adapter), but another version with a very expensive graphics card (about another two million lire) called EGA (Enhanced Graphics Adapter) with sixteen colors was also available. The PC was sold in minimum configuration with 512KB RAM but it was expandable up to 768KB. The next model, the Philips NMS 9100 AT 286 (also known as Philips P3204), had an Intel 80286 microprocessor. It was supplied with Philips DOS 3.0 in Italian and a GW-BASIC interpreter from Philips with answers to error messages in Italian. The software features most included a DOS version of the Philips EASE GUI (source, traslated from Italian: https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philips_NMS_9100_PC).

