$info=nws1250
$mame
0.280 [Raúl Cortés]

NWS-1250, Sony, 1990

Professional workstation from the 1980s, designed for technical and development environments, featuring a 32-bit architecture with a Motorola 68020 processor and the UNIX (NEWS-OS) operating system. Equipped with a high-resolution monochrome display, an ergonomic keyboard, and mouse support, it delivered cutting-edge computing power for its time—perfect for programming, CAD, word processing, and networking applications. The workstation includes serial/parallel ports, expansion slots, and network connectivity, all housed in a sleek, modular design typical of Japanese workstations from the pre-Windows NT era. Aimed at engineers, researchers, and businesses, it stands as a prime example of a high-end computer focused on productivity and reliability in professional settings.

