$info=odyssey2
$mame
0.62 [PeT, Dopefish Justin]

Odyssey 2 (US), Magnavox, 1979

The Magnavox Odyssey?, known in Europe as the Philips Videopac G7000, in Brazil as the Philips Odyssey, in the United States as the Magnavox Odyssey? and the Philips Odyssey?, and also by many other names, is a video game console released in 1978. In the early 1970s, Magnavox was an innovator in the home video game industry. They succeeded in bringing the first home video game system to market, the Odyssey, which was quickly followed by a number of later models, each with a few technological improvements (Magnavox Odyssey Series). In 1978, Magnavox, now a subsidiary of North American Philips, released the Odyssey?, its new second-generation video game console (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnavox_Odyssey%C2%B2).

DRIVER:
philips/odyssey2.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
videopac.xml

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon, machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection), Control Panel, Flyer and Marquee.

WIP:
- 0.264: Correct 0xc00-0xfff rom mirroring [hap].
- 0.224: Changed description to 'Odyssey 2 (US)'. BugFix#07697 [Graphics] (odyssey2.cpp) odyssey2 [qbert]: text and cubes appear incorrectly [hap].
- 0.155: Converted the driver to use slot devices for cartridges, removed The Voice add-on from the main system and emulated it as a passthru cart instead. Now, if you want to enjoy speech in odyssey/videopac games, you must launch emulation with mess odyssey2 -cart voice -cart2 gamename (the -cart2 switch becomes available when "voice" is mounted in the first cartslot) [Fabio Priuli].
- 0.148u2: Further palette improvements [Dr. Venom].
- 0.148u1: Improved palette [Dr. Venom]. Added preliminary lists to Enterprise 64/128, odyssey2 and G-7400, Interact and Kaypro II [LoganB].
- 0.146u2: Fixed noise channel [Justin Kerk, Kevtris].
- 0.145u4: Changed color of background grid color 0 to match sprite color 0 (Fixes KTAA title screen). Fixed odyssey2_video_w so that m_o2_vdc.reg[] is always updated (Fixes Blockout). Changed quad character generation so character height is always taken from 4th character (KTAA level 2) [Dan Boris].
- 0.144u3: Fixed banking regression affecting Kill the Attacking Aliens [Justin Kerk].
- 0.137: Fixed shape of $ character (bug #1582) [Justin Kerk].
- 0.135: Fixed a multisession issue (bug #1517) [Harmony].
- 0.124: Rewrote video code to use scanline-based rendering (fixes Killer Bees intro). Made sound pitch depend on system clock instead of -samplerate (bug #1519). Added support for The Voice! add-on. Added support for larger ROM format used by Kill The Attacking Aliens. Added PAL support for Videopac and C52 [Wilbert Pol].
- 0.114: Major update: banked carts, drawing, and hardware collision detection now all work much better [telengard].
- 0.84: Cleaned up driver and fixed regression introduced in MESS 0.79 (bug #564) [Nate Woods].
- 0.62: Readded Magnavox Odyssey?' system.
- 0.37b15: Removed Magnavox Odyssey?' system.
- 0.37b13: PeT and Dopefish Justin added 'Magnavox Odyssey? driver.

$end
$info=odyssey3
$mame
0.148u2 [Rene van den Enden]

Odyssey 3 Command Center (US, prototype), Philips, 1983

Magnavox intended the Odyssey? to be the follow-up to its somewhat successful 1978 Odyssey? console. Under its hood would have been an Americanized version of the Philips Videopac G7400 (1983), which actually found release in Europe. The G7400 preserved compatibility with Odyssey? games while allowing better graphics for an entirely new line of software. Just as the Odyssey? was set for launch, the video game crash of 1983 ruined everybody's plans, and the second Odyssey sequel never made it to market (source: http://www.pcmag.com/slideshow/story/264435/10-unreleased-video-game-consoles/5).

