$info=olivpc1
$mame
0.221 [Carl, rfka01]

Prodest PC 1, Olivetti, 1987

The Olivetti Prodest PC 1, often called PC1 on unofficial sources, is an IBM compatible personal computer marketed in late 1987-early 1988 by Olivetti. It was aimed primarily at the home computer market, educational or semi-professional, with relatively low price and not excellent capacity for its time. It is the third and last model with the brand of the subsidiary Olivetti Prodest, after the PC 128 and the PC 128s, and has no compatibility with the previous ones. It is based on the 8 MHz NEC V40 (variant of the NEC V20) microprocessor and on a graphics controller that emulates the CGA graphics, and is equipped with 512 kB of RAM. The computer consists of a single block with keyboard and integrated 3.5 "floppy disk drive, with the possibility of integrating a second floppy disk drive or a hard disk as well. The MS operating system was initially supplied as software. -DOS 3.20, the GW-BASIC 3.20 development environment and the Lode Runner video game. To remain in line with the economic and less professional nature of PC 1 compared to other compatible IBM, Olivetti published a library of application software designed for PC 1, inexpensive and not very sophisticated. Otherwise the high-end products in circulation for MS-DOS, such as dBase, Lotus 1-2-3 or Microsoft Word, cost more than the PC 1 itself and were far too powerful for the needs of the typical PC 1 user and for the capabilities of the car. One of the first releases in this sense was the Easy Working series, produced by the American Spinnaker and translated into Italian and adapted to PC 1 by Olivetti. The series includes The Filer (database), The Writer (word processor) and The Planner (spreadsheet), which sold for only 29,000 lire each (source, traslated from Italian: https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olivetti_Prodest_PC_1).

DRIVER:
olivetti/olivpc1.cpp

