$info=pc1640
$mame
0.37b5 [Peter Trauner]

PC1640 SD, Amstrad plc, 1987

The Amstrad PC 1640 was first made in 1986. It has a Intel 8086 processor running at 8 MHz and 640KB of system RAM. It was sold under the name PC 6400 in the US and PC1640 in Europe. At the time, it was a good cheap alternative to an IBM PC and was as good as 100% compatible. The main power supply was not in the system case, but inside the monitor (source: http://www.aoaforums.com/frontpage/computer-hardware-58/324-the-aoa-computer-museum.html?start=13).

