$info=pc40iii
$mame
0.192 [rfka01]

PC 40-III, Commodore Business Machines, 1988

Released in 1989 the Commodore PC40-III is a home/business desktop computer in the range of Commodore International's IBM PC compatible line. It's an IBM PC AT 286 clone running at 12 MHz, it came with an 40 MB hard drive, and usually an 5.25? or optionally an 3.5? HD floppy drive and 1 MB RAM (640 KB base/384 KB hi-mem) and a socket for optional FPU. It also got VGA graphic integrated on the motherboard. Background history: In 1984 Commodore signed a deal with Intel to second source manufacture the Intel 8088 CPU used in the IBM PC. In 1984, the first model in the IBM PC compatible line was released, the PC-1. After Commodore abandoned their own business line of Computers, the CBM-II series that was first released in 1982 to take over for the PET/CBM-series of computers (source: https://rclassiccomputers.com/2018/09/13/commodore-pc40-iii/).

