$info=pc9801rs
$mame
0.142 [Angelo Salese, Carl]

PC-9801RS, NEC, 1989

The NEC PC-9801, or PC-98 series, is a Japanese 16-bit microcomputer manufactured by NEC from 1982 onwards. The platform established NEC's dominance in the Japanese personal computer market, and by 1999, more than 18 million PC-98 units had been sold. The PC-98 is different from the IBM PC in many ways; for instance, it uses its own 16 bit C-Bus instead of the ISA bus; BIOS, I/O port addressing, memory management, and graphics output are also different. However, localized MS-DOS or Windows will still run on PC-9801s. The PC-9801RS as CPU 80386SX 16-20MHz with two floppy-disk da 5" 2HD/2DD (sources: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NEC_PC-9801 and http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/NEC_PC-9801).

