$info=pcw8512
$mame
0.37b5 [Kev Thacker]

PCW8512, Amstrad plc, 1985

A micro rather special as it is almost exclusively dedicated to word processing! Amstrad had seen many people use their microwave for personal applications, games without programming or Basic. This computer was done to them. The PCW8512 has no operating system in ROM, so you use a floppy disk, the famous three-inch format so expensive at the time, to find directly into a system of typing a text or open a file containing text. Very simple, the general public will appreciate. Note that you can also use Basic on the machine, but it was rarely used.
The machine follows the PCW8256 and exited after a few months, providing top of the range, as its name suggests, the PCW8512 has more RAM, but also two floppy drives, so a reading of 720 disks kB and other improvements of second order (source, traslated from french: http://www.obsolete-tears.com/amstrad-pcw8512-machine-154.html).

