$info=pimps
$mame
0.136 [Robbbert]

P.I.M.P.S., Henry Colford, 197?

One of the first 'substantial' bits of code I wrote was a tiny editor and assembler which fit into a 2K (2716) EPROM, and allowed you to enter assembly text in memory and assemble into a working binary program elsewhere in memory. This was very primitive: the editor worked with hexidecimal line numbers and supported only 255 lines. The assembler had no directives other than EQU, DB, DW and END however it did support the entire 8080 instruction set. This little package marked the very beginning of my career in embedded systems development tools (source: http://www.classiccmp.org/dunfield/pimps/index.htm).

