$info=poisk1
$mame
0.137 [Miodrag Milanović]

Poisk-1, Electronmash, 1989

Poisk (Russian: ??????, "The Search") is an IBM-compatible computer built by KPO Electronmash in Kyiv, Ukrainian SSR during the Soviet era. It is based on the K1810VM88 microprocessor, a clone of the Intel 8088. Developed since 1987 and released in 1989, it was the most common IBM-compatible computer in the Soviet Union. The basic version did not include an expansion module for parallel or serial ports for connecting a printer, mouse or other devices. It was not fully IBM compatible, and its performance lagged behind the IBM XT (source: https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisk_(computer)).

