$info=premiere
$mame
0.259 [hap, Berger]

Elite Premiere, Fidelity Electronics International, 1992

The last new release from Fidelity on the German market in 1992 was the Elite Premiere for 1,998 DM. As the name suggests, this was the world's first "two-brain chess computer". In him, two souls beat in one chest, namely the programs of the former "arch enemies" Elite Version 2 and the world champion Mephisto Vancouver. You can also call something like this “reunification at the chess computer level”! On the outside, it resembled the previously released Elite versions like an egg, only with the difference that you could choose between these two programs using a small switch on the front. Inside, a 68000 processor worked at 16 MHz, but the program size logically grew to 256 KB. Unfortunately, there was no way for experimental freaks to transfer the current board position to the other program in the middle of a game. So you had to re-enter the position completely (source, traslated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php/Fidelity_Elite_Premiere).

