Prose 2000/2020 v3.4.1, Telesensory Systems Inc/Speech Plus, 1987

The "Prose 2000" and its successor, the "Prose 2020," are historic and sophisticated standalone text-to-speech (TTS) systems featuring highly integrated hardware, first introduced to the market in 1982 by the U.S. company Speech Plus Inc. (originally a spin-off of Telesensory Systems Inc.). These devices hold a prominent place in the history of assistive technology: the processing engine of the Prose 2000/2020 is the same technology that gave scientist Stephen Hawking his voice back (he initially used this very technology before switching to the later CallText ISA cards).

