$info=proteus3
$mame
0.167 [Robbbert]

Proteus III, Proteus International, 1978

In 1978 the French company Proteus International designs three computers little known today: -the Proteus III A, B -the Proteus III E. Very little information is available about this company, we just know that it was located at 53 rue de Paris 92100 Boulogne Billancourt, from 1978 to 1980, and then changed its name and place of implatation from 1980 to 1982 to become Proteus Computers in Saint Ouen. Its technical director of 1978-1982, Mr Luc Baticle, also designed Proteus III, tells us that the first model in the range, the Proteus III, was sold nearly 400 copies from resellers and described in a kit a hobbyist magazine. The magazine in question is none other than the bimonthly Micro Systems and kit machine is the Micro-Systems 1 (note: we will call "MS1") described in items numbers 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 dating from November to December 1978 to July-August 1979. Its board and component layout are very similar to Proteus III A and B. In parallel, Proteus III A models, B and MS1 initially receive a Basic Basic 8k then a 14K. According to Mr Marc Legardeur, Basic 14K was developed by Thomson (note: we think it is the division of Thomson CSF) and marketed by a small ERCEE named IT company where he was employed for two years. Its role as he was told to "drop start" the MS1 assembled by readers of the magazine Micro Systems. He is also the editor of the manual ERCEE Basic 14K of these machines. The link between Proteus machinery IIIA, B and MS1 is still fuzzy, it's hard to know which came first. Mr Marc D'apès Legardeur, the editor of Micro Systems magazine, Dave Habert had had an agreement with Proteus International to offer the machine kit (source, traslated from French: http://www.proteus-international.fr/).

DRIVER:
skeleton/proteus3.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Preliminary
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

