$info=ptpc110
$mame
0.286 [yyzkevin]

Palm Top PC-110 (Japan), International Business Machines, 1995

The IBM Palm Top PC-110 handheld computer, produced in 1995, was an ultra-compact portable device (subnotebook). It featured a clamshell design with a 4.7-inch monochrome LCD display (640×200), a compact integrated keyboard, and weighed approximately 600 g. Powered by a NEC V30HL processor (a clone of the Intel 8086) running at 7.91 MHz, it came equipped with 1 MB of RAM, expandable up to 2 MB, and storage via SRAM/Flash memory cards (PCMCIA Type II slot). The operating system, MS-DOS 5.0, was built into ROM. The device included RS-232 serial ports, an IrDA infrared interface for wireless data transfer, and power supplied by AA batteries (providing up to 10 hours of operation). It was designed for mobile professionals who needed to manage schedules, perform lightweight spreadsheets, and handle basic communication tasks. At the time, it represented an innovative solution, predating the modern handheld devices that would later emerge with Windows CE and Palm OS PDAs.

