$info=px4
$mame
0.130 [Dirk Best]

PX-4, Epson, 1985

The Epson PX-4 (HC-40 or HX-40) is a portable CP/M based computer introduced in 1985. The screen was 40×8 characters physical, but 80×25 or 40×50 virtual, making it almost compatible with the Epson PX-8 Geneva. It was operated from a NiCD battery pack or four penlight batteries. It was targeted as successor of the Epson HX-20 portable, which was very popular with field engineers. Another feature of the PX-4 was its high modularity. Inheriting the ROM capsules from the Epson PX-8 Geneva, it added 'a cartridge bay (similar but incompatible with the Epson HX-20), for which Epson offered several printers, micro-cassette drive, modem, EPROM writer, DMV, RAM and ROM cartridges. Third parties could make custom cartridges. The modem, EPROM writer and DMV needed user programs. The system allowed for BIOS extensions (User BIOS) to add system support for the extension. Other features were the Serial and RS232 port, barcode reader interface like with the Epson PX-8 Geneva. New were a cassette port and parallel printer port (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epson_PX-4).

