0.194 [Sean Riddle, incog]

Tetris (Radica, Arcade Legends TV Game), Radica (licensed from Elorg / The Tetris Company), 2004

Radica: continues on its way by surfing on the retro wave. This time, it is the legendary Tetris that is proposed. This small stand-alone console plugging into the television is entirely dedicated to the game invented by the Russian Alexei Patjitnov. Like most machines of this kind, the box is very attractive, but quite difficult to unpack properly. The console comes in the form of two joysticks made of colored squares with a large red square button that allows you to direct and rotate the blocks. Everything has a look that sticks pile hair to the game: it's delightfully kitsch. Once the four batteries type LR6 inserted and the scart connected, we can finally try the game. It is nice to see that Alexei Patjitnov is credited with hosting as creator of the game. After the difficulties encountered in the 1980s, it is not a small feat. Fortunately, since 1996, the man has managed to recover the rights of his creation. Radica logo: past, the famous building famous Tetris players appears in a rotation nice enough for a machine of this kind. Graphically, we feel that the machine is not a clone of NES. The graphics are a bit thinner and have a much more elaborate color palette. Unfortunately, the color drool a lot because of the output scart is very average quality (source, translate from French: http://www.obsolete-tears.com/radica-arcade-legends-tetris-machine-303.html).

