$info=rm480z
$mame
0.182 [Robbbert]

LINK 480Z (set 1), Research Machines, 1981

The LINK 480Z was an 8-bit microcomputer produced by Research Machines Limited in Oxford, England, during the early 1980s. The 480Z used a Z80 microprocessor with up to 256 KB of bank-switched RAM. The system could be used as a stand-alone unit with cassette-based storage and the BASIC programming language run from ROM, or it could boot CP/NOS (a network version of CP/M) over a local area network from a file server. When fitted with an optional external floppy disk drive the system could boot the CP/M operating system directly (source: https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/LINK_480Z).

