$info=rpc700
$mame
0.155 [Curt Coder]

Risc PC 700, Acorn, 1994

The RiscPC (stylised with a half-space as Risc PC, also referred to as Risc PC and codenamed Medusa) was Acorn Computers's next generation RISC OS/Acorn RISC Machine computer, launched on 15 April 1994, which superseded the Acorn Archimedes. The Acorn PC card and software allows PC compatible software to be run. Like the Archimedes, the RiscPC continued the practice of having the RISC OS operating system in a ROM module. RiscPC augmented the ROM-based core OS with a disk-based directory structure containing configuration information, and some applications which had previously been kept in ROM. At the 1996 BETT Educational Computing & Technology Awards, the machine was awarded Gold in the hardware category. 1995 - 40 MHz ARM710 CPU upgrade and RiscPC 700 model launched (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RiscPC).

