The Hitachi SH7091 is a microcontroller belonging to the SuperH (SH) family, a series of RISC architectures developed by Hitachi, and later operated in a joint venture with Renesas Technology (derived from the merger of the semiconductor divisions of Hitachi, Mitsubishi Electric, and NEC). In particular, the SH7091 is part of the SH7000 series, which represents one of the first generations of the SuperH family designed for high-performance embedded applications.

