$info=sh7709s
$mame
0.277 [smf]

Hitachi SH7709S, <unknown>, ????

The Hitachi SH7709S is a 32-bit embedded microprocessor, belonging to the SuperH family, and specifically to the SH-2 series . It is an improved variant of the SH7709, designed for high-performance, low-power embedded applications, and was used in many industrial, automotive devices and control technologies in the 1990s and early 2000s.

DRIVER:
devices/cpu/sh/sh7709s.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

WIP:
- 0.287: Update bank conflict precharge penalty timing (#15087): Handle the precharge of the last active bank in the background and only pay the penalty on a bank conflict and only pay the remainder of the wait cycles that haven't elapsed since the last access; update the read->write wait penalty time for dirty writeback eviction since wcr1 should contain that value; fix LRU cache replacement updates (#15098). Only update LRU values greater than the current LRU value on cache hit; fix bug in DRC memory accessor using clobbered IO reg (#15074). Fix a shift error in the wcr1 register values Add bcr2 plumbing for determining cache line fetch reads based off BCR2. Area 0 is set via external pins so hardcoding the cv1k value, this is typically code space though so it only affects timing during copy of code to main memory; convert the cpu cycles to bus cycles properly (#15078) [Jesus Ramos].
- 0.277: smf added 'Hitachi SH7709S' device.

ROMSET:
None required.

$end
$info=sh7750
$mame
0.277 [smf]

Hitachi SH7750, <unknown>, ????

The Hitachi SH7750 is a 32-bit embedded microprocessor, belonging to the SuperH family, and is one of the most advanced models in the SH-4 series, introduced by Hitachi in the 1990s. This processor was designed for applications requiring high performance in areas such as multimedia, automotive systems, video game consoles, and industrial devices. The SH7750 was distinguished by the inclusion of a floating-point unit (FPU), integrated caches, memory management unit (MMU) support, and good integration with external peripherals, making it suitable for both embedded operating systems and light graphics processing tasks.

