$info=snwrite
$mame
0.170 [hap, Sean Riddle, Lord Nightmare, Kevin Horton, plgDavid(David Viens)]

Speak & Write (UK), Texas Instruments, 1982

Think about the original Speak & Spell without a display. A slightly different version was sold in United Kingdom as Speak & Write. The Speak & Spell project was started in the year 1976 and created with the TMC0280 the first one-chip LPC speech synthesizer. Later refinements to the Speak & Spell chips resulted in the TMS5100, 5200 and 5220 Voice Synthesis Processors (VSP) for use in commercial products needing synthetic speech voice output from digitally-stored words and phrases. Speech data was stored in in up to sixteen 128K ROM chips (TMC0350). Blue version of the Speak & Spell Compact sold in the United Kingdom (source: http://www.datamath.org/Speech/SpeaknWrite.htm).

