$info=sprachmg
$mame
0.251 [Dirk Best, natarii, Crypto Museum]

Gerät 32620 (Sprach/Morsegenerator), Institut für Kosmosforschung, 1985

Secret services must always be able to communicate with their own agents in covert ways. Strongly sealed border systems, as they existed during the Cold War, limit the possibility of a secret exchange of information. Many agents used special radios that were dropped behind enemy lines. However, owning and operating a radio always carried the risk of exposure. So that agents around the world could receive messages without having to hide a special device, secret services sent encrypted information via number transmitters. From the 1980s, the Stasi used voice Morse generators to announce the long cryptic columns of numbers. Such a voice Morse generator is part of the collection of the German Spy Museum (source, traslated from German: https://www.deutsches-spionagemuseum.de/sammlung/sprach-morse-generator-geraet-32620).

