$info=sx1010
$mame
0.234 [Plamen Mihaylov]

SX1010, Casio, 1987

Programmable pocket computer manufactured by Casio in the early 1980s (approximately 1981–1982). This handheld device features a 24-character LCD display, a miniaturized alphanumeric keyboard, and BASIC programming capabilities. It comes equipped with 1.3 KB of RAM, expandable via optional modules, and is battery-powered for portable use. Designed for scientific calculations, data management, and simple programmable applications, it was intended for students, engineers, and professionals as an advanced computing tool prior to the advent of the first laptops. The compact design is made of black and gray plastic, complete with a rigid carrying case—a hallmark feature of Casio’s pocket computers from that era.

