$info=t1000
$mame
0.283 [Vas Crabb]

Tandy 1000, Tandy Radio Shack, 1984

Desktop computer released in 1984, designed as an affordable and IBM PC-compatible alternative. Based on the Intel 8088 processor, it offers enhanced graphics compared to the standard IBM model thanks to the Tandy Video II mode (16 colors at 320×200) and three-voice audio via a programmable sound generator chip. It includes joystick ports, support for 5.25" floppy disks, and compatibility with a wide range of MS-DOS software and games from that era. Aimed at home users and small businesses, it quickly gained popularity for its balance of performance, multimedia capabilities, and affordable price.

