$info=ti99_224
$mame
0.128 [Raphael Nabet]

TI-99/2 BASIC Computer (24 KiB ROM), Texas Instruments, 1983

The TI-99/2 BASIC Computer was made to compete with the Timex Sinclair. The 99/2 only displayed black/white. It used a 16-bit high speed processor, 4.2K of memory, and a membrane keyboard usable by touch typists. The 99/2 could only run certain cartridges and tapes, the tapes gave a short introduction to the BASIC computer while the cartridges originally avalable for $19.95 each helped the user become familiar with BASIC. The cartridge names were, "Introduction to Programming" which plugged in the back of the console and immediatly turns the machine into an interactive teacher showing you how to communicate with it (source: http://www.99er.net/992.html).

