$info=tipc
$mame
0.287 [shattered]

Professional Computer, Texas Instruments, 1983

The Texas Instruments Professional Computer (TIPC), a professional computer launched in 1983 as a business-oriented alternative to the IBM PC. It was based on a 5-MHz Intel 8088 processor and featured a proprietary architecture that wasn't fully compatible with the IBM PC. The system included a customized version of MS-DOS and integrated office software (a word processor and a spreadsheet). It boasted an elegant design with a beige case, an ergonomic separate keyboard, and expansion options via a proprietary bus. The TIPC offered enhanced graphics compared to the IBM CGA (640×400 monochrome) and supported 5¼-inch floppy disk drives. Despite its advanced features, the TIPC failed commercially due to its lack of compatibility with the growing IBM PC ecosystem and its high price. It represents an ambitious but isolated attempt by TI in the professional PC market before the company’s eventual withdrawal from the consumer hardware sector.

