0.157 [hap, Sean Riddle]

SR-16, Texas Instruments, 1974

The electronic slide rule SR-16 was the last calculator using the extreme wedge shaped housing of the SR-10 and SR-11. It was superseded by the SR-16 II in a more conventional housing within one year. With the SR-40 prototype an upgraded version of the SR-16 II was developed but never introduced to the market. The colorful desing of the SR-11 was dropped for a style similar to the later SR-50A. Technically the calculator was very similar to its predecessors, one interesting feature could be found in the higher accuracy of some calculations. If you are interested in the calculating accuracy of scientific calculators, don't miss the Calculator forensics (source: http://www.datamath.org/Sci/WEDGE/sr-16.htm).

DRIVER:
handheld/hh_tms1k.cpp

