$info=ts802
$mame
0.147u3 [Al Kossow, Robbbert]

TS802, Televideo, 1982

In 1982, Télévidéo was one of the first companies selling passive video terminals. These devices were used as monitors/keyboards for mainframes. Their major competitor was Digital and its VT100 terminal, which became the reference model and was later on copied by several companies. The same year, Digital and Televideo had the same idea: to convert their video terminal into a business computer. The digital solution was called the VT-180. Televideo offered their solution under the name TS-802. The TS-802 was a traditional Z80 and CP/M based' system. There were two versions: one equiped with double 5.25'' disk drives, and another with a 10 MB hard disk (TS-802H). Like all CP/M systems manufacturers of the time, Televideo offered a free software suite called TeleSolutions. It included the two MicroPro editor bestsellers: WordStar (wordprocessor) and CalcStar (agenda/spreadsheet) (source: http://www.old-computers.com/museum/computer.asp?c=610).

DRIVER:
televideo/ts802.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Preliminary
Color: Good
Sound: Preliminary
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon and machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection).

WIP:
- 0.147u3: Al Kossow and Robbbert added skeleton driver of 'TeleVideo TS-802' system.

ROMSET:
4096 bytes in 1 file / 1.92Kb packed

$end
