$info=us2pfball
$mame
0.184 [hap, Sean Riddle]

Electronic 2-Player Football, U.S. Games Corporation, 1981

Handheld electronic game featuring American football gameplay for two players. Compact plastic case with built-in LED or LCD display (depending on variant), dedicated action buttons for offense/defense, and simple real-time simulation of field position, downs, and scoring. Released in the late 1970s or early 1980s under the U.S. Games brand—a short-lived label used by Quaker Oats for its brief entry into the electronic toy market. No CPU-based intelligence; gameplay driven by discrete logic circuits. Designed as a portable, battery-powered sports-themed diversion for children and teens, typical of pre-microprocessor handhelds that emphasized head-to-head competition over complex graphics or AI.

