$info=utm6502
$mame
0.287 [Stuart Inglis]

6502 interruptible access unit test, MAME, 2026

It is a unit test for MAME to verify the proper functioning of the processor’s interruptible access unit for the 6502. It simulates conditions in which interrupt requests (IRQ/NMI) must be correctly recognized and handled during the execution of specific instructions, especially those that can be interrupted or delayed (e.g., instructions with multiple cycles such as indirect JMP). The test checks the precise timing of interrupt signals, the correct saving of the CPU’s state (PC, P flags) onto the stack, and the proper jump to the appropriate interrupt vector. This test is used to validate the accuracy of the 6502 core within MAME against the actual hardware behavior of the MOS Technology 6502 microprocessor in historical systems such as the Commodore 64, Apple II, or NES consoles.

DRIVER:
virtual/utm6502.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap and Title.

WIP:
- 0.288: Re-add visible PASS msg too. Re-add exit, but do it gracefully. Don't fatalerror when test passed [hap].
- 0.287: Stuart Inglis added 'MAME 6502 interruptible access unit test' system.

ROMSET:
None required.

$end
$info=utrn1024
$mame
0.173 [Felipe Corrêa da Silva Sanches]

Unitron 1024, bootleg (Unitron), 1985

Unauthorized clone of the Apple II+ computer, produced in Brazil by Unitron in the 1980s during a period of import restrictions on computer hardware. Based on a 1-MHz MOS 6502 processor with 48 KB of RAM, expandable to 64 KB, and featuring ROMs identical to those of the original Apple for maximum software compatibility. Includes standard Apple II expansion slots, composite video output, and support for 5¼-inch floppy disk drives. Compact design with a beige case and integrated keyboard. Marketed as an affordable alternative to the original Apple computers for schools and home users in South America. This device represents one of the most faithful clones of the Apple II+, manufactured without Apple’s license and often accompanied by unauthorized logos of the original brand.

DRIVER:
apple/mac128.cpp

