$info=v286c
$mame
0.219 [rfka01]

V286C, Victor, 198?

IBM PC/AT-compatible computer desktop based on a 12 MHz Intel 80286 processor, manufactured by Victor Technologies in the late 1980s. It features a proprietary chipset, a base RAM capacity of 1 MB expandable, a VGA graphics controller, a 40- to 80-MB hard disk, and a 5¼-inch floppy drive. The design comes with either a horizontal or vertical case, AT power supply, and ISA expansion slots. The operating system is MS-DOS 3.x, with an optional Windows 2.x/3.0. Intended for small businesses and offices as an affordable alternative to original IBM models, this computer represents a typical PC clone from the post-IBM AT era, emphasizing software compatibility and competitive pricing in emerging business markets.

