$info=van32t
$mame
0.218 [hap, anonymous]

Mephisto Vancouver 68030, Hegener + Glaser, 1991

The Mephisto tournament machines (hereinafter referred to as TM) were custom-made and were mainly sold by Hobby Computer Centrale Munich on the occasion of the WMCCC Chess Micro World Cup between 1989 and 1992. The wooden housing was based on the proven ESB board. These special devices differed noticeably from the 32-bit "series modules" (Motorola 68020 with 12 MHz and 1 MB RAM), which calculated much slower than the TM. It is estimated that the TM in the "sales version" (Portorose - Vancouver 68030 with 36 MHz) sold between 25 and 50 devices to solvent customers from 1989 to 1992. The appropriate cooling was provided by two fans built into the device (source, traslated from German: https://www.schach-computer.info/wiki/index.php?title=Mephisto_Vancouver_68030).

DRIVER:
hegenerglaser/modular_tm.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Good
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Supported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap and Title.

WIP:
- 0.218: hap and anonymous added 'Hegener + Glaser Mephisto Vancouver 68030' system.

ROMSET:
262144 bytes in 1 file / 113.37Kb packed

$end
$info=van32t8
$mame
0.218 [hap, anonymous]

Mephisto TM Vancouver, Hegener + Glaser, 1991

Mephisto Vancouver, a set of?dedicated chess computer?modules?by?Hegener & Glaser?for their?Mephisto module systems?with a program by?Richard Lang, the successor of?Mephisto Lyon?on the way from?Psion?to?Chess Genius?collecting?World Microcomputer Chess Championship?titles. The seventh title of Richard Lang under the aegis of Mephisto or vice versa at the?WMCCC 1991?in?Vancouver?in the Manufacturers group was per default, since there were no other competitors. In the Software group, Mephisto X aka Vancouver only became third. For a long time, since the?WMCCC 1983, no Lang or Mephisto program won the microcomputer title in the software or commercial group - it was?Ed Schr?der's?Gideon?on an?ARM2?plugin card, followed by?Marty Hirsch's?MChess?on a?PC, heralding an end of an era (source: https://www.chessprogramming.org/Mephisto_Vancouver).

