$info=vectrix
$mame
0.192 [Robbbert]

VX384 Graphics Processor Terminal, Vectrix, 1983

The Vectrix VX 384 was not a stand-alone computer. It was designed as a graphics display, accepting commands from any host through the Centronics or RS-232 port to fill the frame-buffer and drive the external monitor to the indicated resolution. Mainframes to minicomputers to Macs and PC's were used to send graphic info to the VX 384. When the VX 384 was initially released, the PC graphics options were CGA or Hercules monochrome. A smaller model was launched a few time after : the VX128 (VRAM 128 KB and 8 color available per pixel). Vectrix systems were used in a number of medical and manufacturing applications (source: http://www.old-computers.com/museum/computer.asp?c=253).

