$info=vlsi5
$mame
0.214 [rfka01]

286 motherboards with 5-chip VLSI chipset, <unknown>, 198?

Motherboards for Intel 80286 (286) processors equipped with 5-chip VLSI chipsets, primarily manufactured between the mid and late 1980s. These chipsets were produced by VLSI Technology Inc. or by partners such as Chips and Technologies (C&T). The boards typically featured 8/16-bit ISA slots, supported 1–16 MB of RAM (depending on the specific implementation), and required a separate BIOS stored in ROM. Some notable historical examples include boards based on the VLSI 82C206 chipset or OEM configurations featuring VLSI chips paired with C&T components. These architectures represented an economical solution prior to the advent of single-chip chipsets (such as the 82C235) and integrated 386 boards—typical of the era of PC XT/AT-compatible clones before 1990.

