$info=vtechtvsgr
$mame
0.222 [Sean Riddle, Team Europe]

TV Learning Station (VTech, Germany), VTech, 2006

Interactive educational system for children, connected to the TV via RF/composite output. Includes a colorful keyboard with large buttons, a built-in joystick, and slots for educational software cartridges. Based on proprietary 8-bit hardware (often derived from Z80 or 6502 architectures), it runs educational games covering reading, math, logic, and creativity. Features a simple graphic display overlaid on the TV video signal; audio is mono, delivered through an internal speaker or the TV itself. Power is supplied via an external adapter. Marketed in the 1990s–2000s as a home learning tool for children aged 3–8, typical of the pre-digital “edutainment” approach, with content structured around early school curriculum.

