$info=wales210
$mame
0.131 [Roberto Carlos Fernandez Gerhardt, Wilbert Pol]

ES-210, Walther, 199?

Pocket scientific calculator Walther ES-210, manufactured in the 1980s. Features an 8–10-digit LCD display, solar power with a backup battery, basic functions (trigonometric, logarithmic, exponential, root calculations), and single memory. Compact plastic design with rubberized keys and a sliding hard cover. No programmability or graphing capabilities—this is a pure, entry-level scientific calculator intended for high-school students. It represents an economical model from the post-pioneering era of solar calculators, emphasizing reliability, long durability, and straightforward usability without frills.

DRIVER:
nakajima/nakajies.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Preliminary
Color: Good
Sound: Preliminary
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon, machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection) and Control Panel.

WIP:
- 0.131: Roberto Carlos Fernandez Gerhardt and Wilbert Pol added 'Walther ES-210 (not working)' system.

ROMSET:
2097152 bytes in 4 files / 1.32Mb packed

$end
$info=walk386dx
$mame
0.210 [rfka01]

Walkstation 386DX, Triumph-Adler, 1992

IBM PC-compatible laptop based on a 25–33 MHz Intel 80386DX processor, manufactured by Triumph-Adler in the early 1990s. It features 4 MB of RAM, expandable, a 120–200 MB hard disk, a monochrome VGA LCD display (640×480), a full integrated keyboard, and serial/parallel ports. The operating system is MS-DOS 5.x, with an optional Windows 3.1 installation available. The design is rugged, with a case made of reinforced plastic, and it can be powered either via AC adapter or NiCd battery (with limited battery life). Aimed at mobile professionals and businesses, this laptop served as a European alternative to U.S.-made laptops. It represents a complete and reliable 386-based system from the pre-Pentium era, emphasizing software compatibility, expandability, and usability in business environments prior to the widespread adoption of modern portability standards.

DRIVER:
pc/at.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Preliminary
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Imperfect
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
ibm5150.xml, ibm5170.xml, ibm5170_cdrom.xml, generic_cdrom.xml, ibm5170_hdd.xml, midi_flop.xml

RESOURCES:
Snap and Title.

WIP:
- 0.210: rfka01 added 'Triumph-Adler Walkstation 386DX' system.

ROMSET:
131072 bytes in 1 file / 69.58Kb packed

$end
$info=walk386sx
$mame
0.210 [rfka01]

Walkstation 386 SX, Triumph-Adler, 1992

The firm "Triumph-Adler", which originally specialized on production of typewriters, started the manufacture of laptops in 1991. Though "TA" were doing the final assemblage of computers, it produced only 39% of components by it-self. The rest of the parts company was buying in Japan (31%), South Asia (24%), and the USA (4%). The initial price of the first "Walkstation 386 SX" laptop was around 3500 DM, which corresponds to 2.706 ? today (source: http://computermuseum.wiwi.hu-berlin.de/cat.php?id=lapt35&type=Laptops).

DRIVER:
