Xi processor board, Sergey Kiselev, 2012

Experimental motherboard based on the Xi processor, developed by Sergey Kiselev as a personal or academic project during the 2010–2020 decade. Proprietary architecture or RISC-like design with custom-built cores, focused on energy efficiency, low-level parallelism, or exploration of novel computational paradigms. Includes onboard RAM, serial interfaces (UART, SPI, I²C), JTAG debugging, and possibly support for lightweight embedded operating systems. Designed for hardware research, advanced education, or prototyping innovative SoCs. Not intended for commercial markets—this represents an example of independent hardware engineering focused on full design control, specialized optimization, and architectural experimentation outside mainstream industry standards.

