$info=xl25
$mame
0.193 [hap, Sean Riddle]

XL 25, Vulcan Electronics, 1983

The Vulcan Electronics XL 25 was an IBM PC/AT-compatible computer manufactured in the United Kingdom in the late 1980s. Based on a 10-MHz Intel 80286 processor, it featured a 16-bit architecture, ISA expansion slots, support for extended memory (XMS), and the ability to run operating systems such as MS-DOS and OS/2. Designed for small businesses and professional users, it stood out for its excellent value for money and reliability in office environments. Often sold with a VGA monitor and a 5.25-inch floppy disk drive, the XL 25 represented a competitive solution among British PC clones of the time, aiming to replace the more expensive original IBM systems without sacrificing software compatibility.

