$info=z100
$mame
0.143u2 [Minataku, Miodrag Milanović]

Z-100, Zenith Data Systems, 1982

The Z-100 computer is an early personal computer made by Zenith Data Systems (ZDS). It was an alternative to the hardware system that won the marketing shares war in the early to mid 1980s, the IBM PC/XT/AT succession or family of computers. The Z-100 was a pre-assembled version of the Heathkit (recently purchased by Zenith Electronics to become ZDS) H100, which left the final component assembly to early computer hobbyists (source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zenith_Z-100).

DRIVER:
heathzenith/z100.cpp

DRIVER STATUS:
Emulation: Preliminary
Color: Good
Sound: Good
Graphics: Good
Save State: Unsupported

SOFTWARE LIST:
-

RESOURCES:
Snap, Title, Icon, machine picture (in "Cabinet" collection), Control Panel, Flyer and Marquee.

WIP:
- 0.210: Render video through CRTC instead of working around it by caching registers, and map video RAM using PROM module. Added 8253 timer and associated interrupt, cursor, video interrupt, and Centronics port. Converted keyboard to low-level emulation, and added 'keyboard click/beep sound. Corrected numerous clocks [AJR]. Dumped 8041 keyboard controller program [Herb Johnson].
- 0.143u2: Minataku and Miodrag Milanović added skeleton driver of 'Zenith Z-100' system.

ROMSET:
18720 bytes in 8 files / 12.06Kb packed

$end
