$info=z22
$mame
0.222 [Bitsavers]

Z-22 Terminal, Zenith Data Systems, 1984

Smart terminal produced by Zenith Data Systems in the early 1980s. An alphanumeric device featuring a 12-inch monochromatic CRT display (typically green or amber) with a standard resolution of 24 lines × 80 columns. Designed to connect to mainframes, minicomputers, or Unix systems via an RS-232 serial port, it offered VT100 emulation and support for programmable function keys. Primarily used in corporate environments, banks, and universities as an interface for remote access to centralized systems, before the widespread adoption of graphical workstations. It featured a compact design with a gray metal chassis and an integrated keyboard—typical of professional terminals of that era. It was not a standalone computer but rather a peripheral device dedicated to communication with remote hosts.

